    "predictive": "good",
    "witness": "THE STRONGEST MEASUREMENT PROFILE IN THE REGISTER, alongside HIV and PKU, and it was built because the alternative was unbearable. Serological screening by card agglutination or rapid test, parasitological confirmation by microscopy, and staging by lumbar puncture — a complete pathway, deployed by mobile teams to villages that have no clinic. PROGNOSTIC is `good` in a bleak way: untreated infection kills essentially everyone, so what it will do is not in question. PREDICTIVE is `good` because STAGE DETERMINES TREATMENT, and the stage is measurable — which is what the lumbar puncture was for. AND HERE IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. That lumbar puncture existed because stage 2 disease required melarsoprol, an arsenical that killed three to five percent of the people given it, so nobody would administer it without proof it was needed. Fexinidazole treats both stages orally, and with it much of the staging requirement falls away. **A better drug abolished a diagnostic procedure**, which is the hepatitis C pattern — a measurement burden closed by better treatment rather than by a better test — applied to an invasive procedure rather than to an assay. THE ENDGAME PROBLEM IS NOT IN THESE RATINGS. A screening test with excellent specificity still produces mostly false positives when prevalence falls to a handful per hundred thousand, which is the situation success creates. The test did not get worse; the population did. Nothing in this block can say so — see gaps.md #56, and note that schistosomiasis hit the same wall from the other direction."
