    "what": "Now: fexinidazole, ten days of oral tablets, with nausea, vomiting and headache that resolve. Nothing permanent. NECT, still used for severe stage 2 gambiense disease, is a fortnight of infusions plus oral nifurtimox — heavier, and survivable. READ THE `moved` BLOCK, BECAUSE THIS RATING IS FIVE YEARS OLD. For sixty years the treatment for stage 2 disease was melarsoprol, an organoarsenical from 1949 administered in propylene glycol and described by patients as fire in the veins. It caused a reactive encephalopathy in five to ten percent of recipients, of whom roughly half died — so the treatment killed something like three to five percent of the people it was given to, and was used anyway for six decades, because the alternative was a disease that killed all of them. That is the largest toll reduction recorded anywhere in this register: `catastrophic` to `minor`, without any change in capability. It was curable before and it is curable now.",
