    "what": "Stage 2 disease is an encephalitis. The parasite crosses the blood-brain barrier and produces the sleep-cycle inversion that names the disease, along with movement disorder, and neuropsychiatric and cognitive change. Cure stops the infection; it does not reliably undo what the inflammation did. Persistent neurological and psychiatric deficits after successful treatment of late-stage disease are reported — cognitive impairment, sleep disturbance, personality change — and are the reason staging mattered for more than drug choice. RATED WITH LOW CONFIDENCE AND STATED AS SUCH. The literature here is small case series rather than cohorts, and a disease with eight hundred cases a year does not generate the follow-up studies that would settle it. `major` describes the harm when it happens, per the schema's own rule, and the low incidence carries how often. Treated in stage 1, before the parasite reaches the brain, there is no residue at all — which is the whole content of the `window` below.",
