     "kind": "policy",
     "blocks": "sustaining surveillance after the disease stops being visible",
     "what": "THE ENDGAME BLOCKER, AND IT IS THE ONE THIS RECORD EXISTS TO SHOW. HAT was down to a few thousand cases around 1960. Colonial-era screening programmes were wound down through independence, war and structural adjustment, and the disease returned to hundreds of thousands of cases by the 1990s. The current achievement is a repeat of a position that was previously held and then lost. What is required now is money and institutional attention for a disease that produces eight hundred cases a year, in countries with a great many more pressing problems, for at least a decade after it appears finished. Every incentive — political, financial, and the register's own burden-weighted arithmetic — argues for spending the money elsewhere. This is not a knowledge problem or a delivery problem in the ordinary sense. It is the problem of paying to keep a zero at zero, and it is invisible to a schema that measures gaps. See gaps.md #57.",
