     "blocks": "knowing whether elimination of transmission is achievable at all",
     "what": "Two specific unknowns, both bearing on the 2030 target rather than on treating any individual. First, the reservoir. If asymptomatic carriers or skin-dwelling trypanosomes sustain transmission invisibly, then a strategy built on screening blood will drive incidence very low and never to zero — and the programme will not be able to tell the difference between success and a hidden pool until cases return. Second, there is no vaccine and antigenic variation makes one close to precluded: the parasite switches its surface coat continuously, which is exactly the thing a vaccine cannot chase. This is one of the few records where the absence of a vaccine is a statement about the biology rather than about investment. `scale: null` — no priced path.",
