     "blocks": "a supply of drugs for a disease with eight hundred patients",
     "what": "Documented, historically vivid, and currently latent in a way worth distinguishing. The historical case is the sharpest market-failure story in this register. Eflornithine, which worked against stage 2 gambiense disease, was commercially unattractive and production was in doubt — and its availability was sustained in part because the same molecule turned out to have a cosmetic use in wealthy markets. A treatment for a fatal African disease was kept alive by a facial hair cream. Nobody designed that, and no register should let it pass without recording it. The current case is prospective. Fexinidazole exists because a nonprofit product development partnership built it with a manufacturer. Once incidence approaches zero, the question is who continues to make and stockpile drugs for a few hundred patients a year, indefinitely, in case the disease returns — which is the same improvised-institution dependency as Chagas, tuberculosis and visceral leishmaniasis, at an even smaller scale.",
