   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "good",
    "witness": "THE STRONGEST MEASUREMENT PROFILE OF ANY RECORD HERE THAT IS STILL KILLING PEOPLE AT SCALE, and that is the point of reading it. DIAGNOSTIC is `objective` and genuinely good: sputum smear, culture as a slow gold standard, and rapid molecular testing that returns a result in hours AND reports rifampicin resistance in the same run. Chest imaging with automated reading extends it further. The gaps are real but narrow — sputum is unreliable in children, in HIV-associated paucibacillary disease and in extrapulmonary TB, and a substantial minority of notified cases are never bacteriologically confirmed. PREDICTIVE is `good`, which almost nothing else in this register achieves. Drug susceptibility testing answers \"will this regimen work in this patient\" directly, by testing the organism rather than inferring from the patient. That is what predictive measurement is supposed to look like, and TB has had it for decades. The failure is coverage, not capability — a large share of notified patients never get a full susceptibility profile — and that is an access problem wearing a measurement problem's clothes. PROGNOSTIC is `partial`, and the missing half sits in latent infection rather than in active disease. Untreated active TB kills roughly half of those who have it, which is well characterised. What nothing predicts is which of the two billion latently infected people will progress. That single absence is what makes preventive therapy a lottery played at the scale of a quarter of humanity — see the LTBI discussion under `intervention`, and gaps.md #47. Read all three together: measurement here is close to as good as medicine gets, and 1.25 million people a year still die. Huntington's showed measurement without capability is useless. TB shows the converse — excellent measurement AND real capability, defeated by delivery. Neither is the lever."
   },
