     "blocks": "knowing what the disease is currently doing",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THE MOST INTENSIVELY MEASURED DISEASE IN HISTORY IS NOW MEASURED WORSE THAN TUBERCULOSIS, AND THE INSTRUMENTS STILL WORK.** Free testing ended, case reporting became voluntary or stopped, genomic sequencing volumes collapsed, and wastewater programmes were defunded in many places. The PCR assay, the antigen test and the sequencing pipeline all still exist. **Almost nobody is running them and almost nowhere reports the result.** The decisions were defensible individually — the emergency ended, the money was needed elsewhere, and surveillance at pandemic intensity is not a permanent condition. The aggregate is that **the register cannot state this record's current burden within an order of magnitude**, and neither can anybody else. **The loss is self-concealing, which is what makes it different from every other blocker here.** A dismantled surveillance system removes the signal that would show it was needed, so the evidence for rebuilding it can only arrive as a surprise. And the same infrastructure — sequencing, wastewater, excess-mortality monitoring — is what would detect the next pathogen, so its value is not principally about this record at all (gaps.md #165). **`h5n1` is the record that depends on it**, and that record's whole argument is about a threat rather than a burden.",
