     "what": "**RECOVERY ANSWERED MORE CLINICAL QUESTIONS THAN THE REST OF THE WORLD COMBINED, AND IT WAS NOT BECAUSE THE UNITED KINGDOM HAD BETTER IDEAS.** It had a health system in which a randomised trial could be embedded in routine care, a one-page consent process, and a willingness to randomise at scale during an emergency. It found dexamethasone in three months, and it **ruled out** hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir-ritonavir, azithromycin, colchicine and convalescent plasma, ending enormous waste. Meanwhile hundreds of trials elsewhere were too small to answer anything and consumed the patients who could have been in a trial that would. **The blocker is that the capability to find out is unevenly distributed and is nobody's disease.** No record in this register is improved by \"build trial infrastructure\", so no record argues for it, and the register cannot express that the highest-return investment of the pandemic was a trial design (gaps.md #164). It generalises immediately: the same absence is why `sepsis` has a hundred failed trials, why `neonatal-conditions` exported interventions whose sign flipped, and why `me-cfs` has almost no evidence base at all.",
