   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "clinical",
    "prognostic": "none",
    "predictive": "partial",
    "witness": "DIAGNOSTIC is `clinical`, AND THAT IS REMARKABLE FOR A BACTERIAL DISEASE WITH A NAMED ORGANISM. The cardinal signs are a skin lesion with definite sensory loss, a thickened peripheral nerve, and acid-fast bacilli on a slit-skin smear — and the smear is negative in paucibacillary disease, which is most of it. There is no serological test that works across the spectrum, no antigen test, and **the organism has never been grown in culture.** Drug susceptibility historically required inoculating mouse footpads and waiting a year; molecular methods have improved that and are not field tools. So the diagnosis rests on a trained pair of eyes and a wisp of cotton wool testing sensation — which is exactly the capability that dispersed when vertical programmes were integrated after 2000. This is the first record in this batch where the diagnostic problem is not a missing device but a missing *person*, and it cannot be fixed by procurement. PROGNOSTIC is `none`, and the gap is where all the disability comes from. Leprosy reactions — type 1 reversal reactions and erythema nodosum leprosum — are immune-mediated episodes that occur during or, crucially, AFTER successful treatment, and they are the principal cause of nerve damage. Nothing predicts who will have one. A patient can be bacteriologically cured and lose a hand afterwards. PREDICTIVE is `partial`: the paucibacillary/multibacillary split determines whether treatment runs six months or twelve, and it is made on lesion count and smear rather than on anything precise."
   },
