   "residue": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "THE DEFORMITY IS NOT THE BACTERIUM EATING FLESH. That misconception is ancient and it matters, because the truth points at an intervention. *M. leprae* infects Schwann cells and damages peripheral nerves. The result is anaesthesia — hands and feet that cannot feel. What follows is unnoticed injury: a burn not felt, a stone in a shoe not felt, an ulcer that is walked on until it reaches bone. Repeated trauma to an insensate limb produces the resorption, contracture and mutilation the disease is known for. Add lagophthalmos and corneal damage from facial nerve involvement, and blindness. Nerve damage does not reverse when the bacilli are killed, and reactions can damage nerves months or years AFTER a bacteriological cure. Three to four million people are living with this, against two hundred thousand new diagnoses a year. AND MOST OF THE PROGRESSION IS PREVENTABLE WITHOUT ANY DRUG. Daily inspection of insensate hands and feet, protective footwear, wound care and simple splinting stop the ulcer-injury-resorption cycle. That is the lymphatic filariasis finding again: **the residue is the treatable part**, and it is treated by self-care training rather than by pharmacology (gaps.md MDT programme is built to deliver.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.2,
     "units": "fraction-of-patients (permanent nerve damage or visible disability) — a reasoned estimate; the WHO grade-2 disability rate at diagnosis alone runs around five to ten percent, and damage accrues during and after treatment",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "window": {
    "what": "The window is nerve damage, and it is the whole of what matters here. Treated before the nerves are injured, leprosy leaves nothing at all: skin lesions resolve, the patient is cured, there is no deformity and no disability. Treated after, the bacilli die and the anaesthesia is permanent, and everything the disease is feared for follows from that anaesthesia rather than from the infection. It is a slow window and an unusually cruel one, for two reasons. Incubation runs two to twenty years, so nobody presents early on suspicion. And the first sign is a patch of skin that does not feel — which is painless, unalarming, and easy to ignore in a disease people have every social reason not to name. Reactions extend the window past cure: a patient discharged as bacteriologically cured can lose nerve function months later, so \"caught in time\" is not settled at the end of treatment.",
    "closes_on": "established peripheral nerve damage — sensory loss, motor weakness, lagophthalmos — which does not recover when the organism is killed",
    "caught_in_time": {
     "value": 0.6,
     "units": "fraction-of-patients (treated before significant nerve damage) — a reasoned estimate; the grade-2 disability rate at diagnosis is the published proxy and understates it, since grade 1 sensory loss is already irreversible",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`established`, with two holes that matter and one fact that is strange enough to state on its own. *Mycobacterium leprae* — and, since 2008, *M. lepromatosis* — infects Schwann cells of peripheral nerves and macrophages of skin. It has undergone extreme reductive evolution: something like half its genome is pseudogenes, and it is so dependent on its host that IT HAS NEVER BEEN GROWN IN CULTURE. Research requires armadillos or mouse footpads. That single limitation shapes everything downstream — no rapid susceptibility testing, slow drug development, and a diagnostic pipeline that never had a laboratory anchor to build on. THE CLINICAL SPECTRUM IS A PROPERTY OF THE HOST. Strong cell-mediated immunity produces tuberculoid disease: few lesions, few bacilli, sharply localised. Weak cell-mediated immunity produces lepromatous disease: many lesions, enormous bacillary loads, widespread involvement. Same organism, opposite diseases, and the Ridley-Jopling spectrum between them is a classification of immune response rather than of pathogen. The holes: transmission is not settled — prolonged close contact and respiratory droplet are strongly favoured — and only a small minority of those exposed ever develop disease, with host genetics implicated and unresolved. Neither is a mystery on the Buruli ulcer scale, and both limit what prevention can be aimed at.",
    "intervention": "`curative`, and it has been since 1982. Multidrug therapy — rifampicin, dapsone and clofazimine — cures, in six months for paucibacillary and twelve for multibacillary disease, with relapse uncommon. It has been provided FREE WORLDWIDE SINCE 1995 through a manufacturer donation, and over sixteen million people have completed it. See `moved` for what it replaced. Dapsone monotherapy took years, produced widespread resistance, and left many patients on treatment indefinitely — so the introduction of MDT moved this record from `suppressive` to `curative`, which is a genuine capability change and the first one this register has recorded on the intervention axis outside hepatitis C. What MDT does not do is repair a damaged nerve, and it does not prevent the reactions that damage them.",
    "prevention": "`prophylaxis`, and recently earned. A SINGLE DOSE OF RIFAMPICIN given to contacts of a new case reduces their subsequent incidence by roughly half — post-exposure prophylaxis, deployable, cheap, and now recommended. BCG gives partial protection as a side effect of being given for tuberculosis. This is one of the few records in the register where prevention is a genuine prophylaxis given to a person rather than an environmental measure, and it is worth noting what it needs: knowing who the contacts are, which requires finding the index case, which requires the clinical expertise that was dispersed after 2000.",
    "ongoing": "`moderate` for treatment — six or twelve months of daily and monthly doses, with monitoring for reactions that may need months of steroids. The rating misses the larger burden entirely, and deliberately, because the axis is defined as the burden of being TREATED. Three to four million people perform daily self-care on insensate limbs for the rest of their lives — inspecting, protecting, dressing — and none of them is under treatment. That is `high` by any reading and it belongs to the residue, not to the therapy."
   },
