     "blocks": "finding cases, in a disease with no laboratory test",
     "what": "THE BINDING CONSTRAINT, AND IT IS A MISSING PERSON RATHER THAN A MISSING DEVICE. Leprosy is diagnosed clinically — a skin patch with definite sensory loss, a thickened nerve, bacilli on a smear that is negative in most cases. The organism cannot be cultured, serology fails across the paucibacillary half of the spectrum, and there is no point-of-care test that works. So diagnosis depends on a clinician who has seen leprosy before and knows to test sensation with a wisp of cotton. That capability was concentrated in vertical programmes, and after the 2000 elimination declaration those programmes were integrated into general health services and the expertise dispersed. A general practitioner in a low-incidence district may never see a case. No procurement fixes this. What would is training, referral pathways and a reason for a health system to maintain competence in something it has been told is eliminated — which is a policy problem wearing a diagnosis problem's clothes.",
