     "blocks": "sustaining a programme against a target that has been declared met",
     "what": "Documented, dated, and the reason this record exists. \"Elimination as a public health problem\" was defined as fewer than one REGISTERED case per 10,000 — registered meaning currently on treatment. Shortening treatment from years of dapsone to six or twelve months of multidrug therapy cut that number mechanically. The target was declared met globally in 2000 and by most endemic countries soon after. What followed is the yaws pattern with better documentation: vertical programmes wound down, integration into general services, loss of specialist capacity, and new case detection flat at around 200,000 a year for two decades. Nothing about the organism changed. The second half of this blocker is legal. Discriminatory provisions persisted in national law long after the disease was curable — grounds for divorce, bars on public office, segregation powers — and some were repealed only in the last decade. A disease whose treatment is free and whose cure is certain is still one people conceal.",
