     "blocks": "predicting reactions, and everything an unculturable organism blocks",
     "what": "Two research problems and one structural handicap. The handicap: *M. leprae* has never been grown in culture. Drug susceptibility testing historically meant inoculating mouse footpads and waiting a year; molecular methods have improved that and there is still no routine phenotypic testing, and no easy platform for developing anything new. The first problem is reactions. Type 1 and type 2 reactions cause most of the nerve damage, they occur during and after successful treatment, and nothing predicts them. A marker that identified who was about to have one would prevent more disability than any new antibiotic. The second is who develops disease at all. Only a small minority of those exposed do; host genetic susceptibility is implicated and unresolved, and an answer would let post-exposure prophylaxis be aimed rather than given to every contact. `scale: null` — no priced path.",
