    "note": "**THIS RECORD IS THE OPPOSITE POLE OF THE REGISTER FROM THE LAST TWO.** Pancreatic cancer runs 510,000 cases against 470,000 deaths. Liver cancer runs 870,000 against 760,000. **Thyroid cancer runs 820,000 against 45,000.** Those three records bound the corpus and nothing about them generalises to each other. Autopsy series find occult papillary thyroid carcinoma in a large share of people who died of other causes — with careful sectioning, reported in substantial fractions of adults. Like prostate cancer, it is close to a normal feature of ageing that occasionally matters. **AND THE DEATHS AND THE DIAGNOSES ARE ALMOST DIFFERENT DISEASES.** The incidence is papillary microcarcinoma. The mortality is anaplastic carcinoma — one of the most lethal human cancers, median survival around half a year — plus radioiodine-refractory differentiated disease and medullary carcinoma. Two percent of the record supplies a large share of the deaths. One genuine incidence increase is not overdiagnosis at all: **childhood thyroid cancer after Chernobyl**, caused by iodine-131 exposure, real and documented and preventable by potassium iodide blockade that was not given in time."
