     "kind": "knowledge",
     "blocks": "the last drug class, against an organism that has taken six",
     "what": "**THE SEQUENCE IS DOCUMENTED, ONE-DIRECTIONAL AND NEARLY COMPLETE.** Sulfonamides, penicillin, tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, cefixime and azithromycin have each been recommended and each been abandoned. **Ceftriaxone is what remains**, resistant isolates have been reported on several continents, and dual therapy was given up in some guidelines because the partner drug had already failed — a retreat to monotherapy on the last effective class. The organism is built for this. **Natural competence means it acquires resistance rather than evolving it**, recombining DNA from commensal *Neisseria* that share the pharynx and have been exposed to every antibiotic their host ever took for anything else. The pharyngeal infection is asymptomatic, untested in most settings, and cured least reliably — so the reservoir, the incubator and the blind spot are the same anatomical site. Zoliflodacin is the first genuinely new class to complete phase 3 in decades. **One new drug against an organism that has taken six is a reprieve.** `scale: null` for the discovery; the development half is priceable and is `no-sponsor`'s problem.",
