   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "clinical",
    "prognostic": "none",
    "predictive": "none",
    "witness": "DIAGNOSTIC is `clinical`, the fourth record in a row to rate so and the fourth for a different reason. Skin scraping and microscopy for mites is objective and misses roughly half of cases, because ordinary scabies carries only ten or fifteen mites on a whole body. Dermoscopy does better in trained hands. So diagnosis rests on the 2020 consensus criteria — burrows, typical distribution, nocturnal itch, affected household contacts — which are explicitly a clinical framework with confirmed, clinical and suspected tiers. PROGNOSTIC is `none`, and it hides the consequential question. Nothing predicts who develops impetigo from scratching, and therefore nothing predicts who is on the path toward streptococcal complications. Nothing predicts who develops crusted scabies either, which matters for everybody else (see the strata). PREDICTIVE is `none` AND THERE IS NO TEST OF CURE, which combine into a genuinely nasty clinical loop. Itch after successful treatment is expected — it is a hypersensitivity reaction to dead mite antigen and persists for weeks. So a patient who still itches cannot be distinguished from a patient who was reinfested, or from one whose mites were resistant. The usual response is to treat again with a topical agent **that is itself an irritant**, which causes itch, which prompts further treatment. That is the second feedback loop in this register after leprosy's stigma cycle (gaps.md #77), and the first that is purely clinical. It is also the fifth record with no test of cure (gaps.md #45), and here the absence is not an inconvenience — it drives real over-treatment."
   },
