   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`partial`, and the rating undersells how much is genuinely known while being honest about the hole. Breast cancer is among the best mechanistically dissected of all cancers: oestrogen receptor signalling, HER2 amplification, BRCA1/2 and homologous recombination deficiency, PIK3CA and TP53, and the intrinsic molecular subtypes. Crucially, several treatments were DESIGNED against known targets rather than found — tamoxifen and aromatase inhibitors against ER, trastuzumab against HER2, PARP inhibitors against BRCA-mutant tumours, CDK4/6 inhibitors against the cell-cycle machinery. That is a mechanism-to-drug chain most of this register lacks entirely. The named holes: what initiates the disease in a given woman is probabilistic rather than causal; why metastatic cells lie dormant for fifteen or twenty years and then reawaken is unexplained, and it is why \"cured\" can only ever be said retrospectively; and there is no way to tell an indolent early cancer from a lethal one at diagnosis, which is the direct cause of the overtreatment above.",
    "intervention": "`curative` at record level because early disease is genuinely cured — but read the strata. Multimodal therapy is the standard: surgery, radiation, and systemic treatment matched to subtype. Metastatic disease is `suppressive` and no line of therapy changes that.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction` for the population — chemoprevention with tamoxifen or an aromatase inhibitor in high-risk women, and the modifiable contributions of alcohol, obesity and physical inactivity. For BRCA1/2 carriers specifically, risk-reducing mastectomy and salpingo-oophorectomy are `prophylaxis` grade, which is a stratum-level fact the prevention axis cannot currently express. Screening is early detection, not prevention, and is recorded in `window`.",
    "ongoing": "`moderate`. Five to ten years of endocrine therapy for the hormone-receptor- positive majority, with discontinuation rates that reflect how unpleasant it is, plus surveillance and the permanent knowledge that late recurrence happens."
   },
