     "to": 0.8,
     "why": "**CHECKPOINT BLOCKADE AND BRAF INHIBITION, IN THE SAME YEAR.** Ipilimumab became the first therapy ever to show a survival benefit in metastatic melanoma; vemurafenib showed that BRAF V600 tumours could be switched off. PD-1 inhibitors followed within four years, then combinations, then adjuvant use in resected disease. For the metastatic stratum this took five-year survival from under a tenth to roughly half — **arguably the largest single-disease survival improvement in modern oncology.** **AND LOOK AT THE NUMBERS ON THIS ENTRY.** Record-level efficacy moves from about 0.65 to about 0.80, because the great majority of melanoma was already cured by cutting it out and the transformation reached a tenth of diagnoses. The largest advance in oncology is a fifteen-point move in a record-level scalar, and the honest version of it is in `strata` where the register does not report it as a change at all. gaps.md #61."
