   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`established`, and the causal chain has a property nothing else in this register has: **the thing that causes the disease is the reason the disease is treatable.** Ultraviolet light produces characteristic DNA damage — pyrimidine dimers — and melanocytes accumulate mutations carrying an unmistakable UV signature. Melanoma ends up with one of the highest mutational burdens of any human cancer. BRAF V600 in roughly half, NRAS in a fifth, NF1 loss in much of the rest; the MAP kinase pathway is switched on in nearly all of it. **And a high mutational burden means a large number of neoantigens** — peptides the immune system has never seen and can be unleashed against. That is why checkpoint blockade works better in melanoma than in almost anything else, and it is why it works so much less well in acral and mucosal melanoma, which are not UV-driven and carry far fewer mutations. The sun caused the disease and then made it visible to the immune system. THE NAMED HOLES ARE TWO. **Nobody can predict who responds to checkpoint blockade** — roughly half do not, and PD-L1 and mutational burden only shift the odds. **And acral and mucosal melanoma are poorly understood**, driven by KIT and other alterations rather than by UV, with no equivalent breakthrough.",
    "intervention": "`curative`, and it has been for a century — a melanoma excised with an adequate margin while it is thin is cured, and that describes most patients. What changed is everything below that line. Before 2011 metastatic melanoma was among the most reliably fatal cancers there was: dacarbazine, approved in 1975, produced responses in about a tenth of patients and never demonstrated a survival benefit; high-dose interleukin-2 produced durable remissions in a handful at severe cost. Now: **combination checkpoint blockade gives roughly half of patients five-year survival, and the curve plateaus.** Adjuvant immunotherapy reduces recurrence in resected node-positive disease. BRAF/MEK inhibition offers rapid control in the mutant half. **The plateau is the interesting object and the schema cannot hold it.** A flat tail means people stopped progressing and stayed stopped, some of them years after therapy ended. That is what cure looks like from inside. It is also what a very long suppression looks like from inside, and the difference only becomes visible with time this treatment has not yet had. The record reads `suppressive` for that stratum on the conservative choice, and the choice is arbitrary — see gaps.md #118.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`, and it is one of the few in this register with a demonstrated population result. Sun protection, shade, clothing, avoiding peak ultraviolet, and not using sunbeds. **Australia has the highest melanoma incidence in the world and has seen incidence begin to fall in younger cohorts** after decades of sustained public campaigning — a prevention effect that took roughly forty years to appear, which is the same politically hopeless lag tobacco control has. Sunbeds deliver a Group 1 carcinogen for cosmetic purposes and remain legal in much of the world; several countries have banned them for minors and a few outright. That is the closest thing melanoma has to a tobacco-control lever and it is a small one, because most exposure is from the sun. It is `risk-reduction` rather than `prophylaxis` because there is nothing to give anybody — the intervention is behaviour, and behaviour sustained over a lifetime against something that feels pleasant.",
    "ongoing": "`low`, and it is the only cancer in this register that can say so. Most patients are cured by an excision and carry skin surveillance — regular self-examination and periodic dermatology review, because a first melanoma is the strongest known risk factor for a second. Patients on checkpoint blockade take it for up to two years and frequently stop, which is unusual among effective cancer therapies and is part of why the cure question is live. Those left with immune-related endocrine failure carry lifelong hormone replacement, which is `toll` rather than `ongoing`."
   },
