     "blocks": "two to three days, in a disease where each day is measured in lives",
     "what": "**THE TEST IS DEFINITIVE AND IT IS TOO SLOW, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE BLOCKER.** A blood culture takes two to three days to flag; time to appropriate antifungal therapy is the strongest modifiable predictor of death in this disease; and so the standard of care is to guess. Empirical antifungals are given to large numbers of ICU patients who do not have candidaemia — with the cost, and the resistance selection, that entails — while the ones who do have it wait. Rapid direct-from-blood detection exists. T2 magnetic resonance identifies *Candida* species within hours; it is expensive, it is deployed in a minority of centres, and it has not displaced culture. **This is the register's cleanest diagnostic gap that is purely about speed** — the test is not missing, not unaffordable in principle, and not hard to interpret. It is late.",
