     "kind": "logistics",
     "blocks": "somebody competent being in the room at the moment of birth",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THE INTERVENTION WITH THE BEST RETURN IN THIS RECORD IS A TRAINED PERSON AND A BAG-VALVE MASK, AND IT CANNOT BE PROCURED.** A baby who does not breathe needs ventilation within sixty seconds. The equipment costs a few dollars and lasts years. **The constraint is that somebody who has been taught the skill, and practised it recently, is standing there** — at a birth that may happen at night, at home, or in a facility with one midwife covering a ward. This is `childhood-pneumonia`'s oxygen problem in its purest form (gaps.md failure mode that is invisible in any procurement report. Resuscitation training decays measurably within months without practice, so it is a recurring operating cost disguised as a one-off intervention — which is exactly the shape #57 says the register cannot value. Add to it warmth, a clean surface, a bilirubin meter and a phototherapy lamp, an oxygen blender and a saturation monitor. Every item is cheap. **The system that keeps all of them working, at three in the morning, is the thing that does not exist.**",
