     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THIRTEEN MILLION PRETERM BIRTHS A YEAR AND NOBODY KNOWS WHY MOST OF THEM HAPPEN.** Spontaneous preterm labour — the majority — has no established mechanism. Intrauterine infection and inflammation, cervical insufficiency, uterine overdistension, and maternal stress and undernutrition are all associated. None of them predicts an individual pregnancy, and the two interventions that work — vaginal progesterone and cervical cerclage — work in narrowly selected women identified by a short cervix or a previous preterm birth, which is a small share of the total. The consequence is that **essentially the entire global response to prematurity is care for the baby afterwards.** That is not a misallocation: it is what you do when you cannot prevent the event. But it means the largest single cause of death in children under five has no preventive programme aimed at the cause, and the register has no way to say that a `prophylaxis` rating is being carried entirely by interventions aimed downstream. **This blocker has no Engpass obstacle and should have one.** \"Why does labour start early\" is a mechanism question cited by exactly one record so far, which is below Engpass's granularity threshold, and it is the largest untagged knowledge blocker in the corpus. `scale: null`.",
