     "blocks": "an ophthalmologist at the cot, on schedule, for ten weeks",
     "what": "**THE BINDING CONSTRAINT, AND IT IS A PERSON RATHER THAN A PRODUCT.** Screening requires someone trained in indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral depression who is willing to examine ventilated infants weekly on a fixed schedule in a neonatal unit. The laser is not scarce, bevacizumab costs very little per dose, and neither matters without the examination. Ophthalmologists able and willing to do this are scarce everywhere and close to absent across the third-epidemic countries. The work is unglamorous, poorly reimbursed, carries real medicolegal exposure, and takes a specialist out of a clinic where they could see forty patients. **A programme running at 90% coverage is not 90% as good as one running at 100%** — the infant missed is the infant blinded — which makes this one of the few records where partial delivery has almost no partial value.",
