    "note": "**INCIDENCE UP, MORTALITY UP MUCH LESS, AND NO SCREENING PROGRAMME ANYWHERE.** The gap is imaging. Abdominal CT and ultrasound became routine for a hundred other indications, and kidneys are large, superficial to the scanner and impossible to miss. Unlike thyroid cancer, the extra detection has bought something. Small renal masses found incidentally and treated are cured, and stage-at-diagnosis has shifted markedly toward localised disease. Unlike colorectal cancer, a substantial share of what is found did not need finding: **roughly a fifth of small renal masses are benign** — oncocytoma, angiomyolipoma — and many of the malignant ones grow at a few millimetres a year in patients in their seventies. Risk factors are smoking, obesity and hypertension, which makes this the **sixth record in the tobacco family** and puts two more queued entities upstream of it. Chronic kidney disease and dialysis raise risk substantially, and acquired cystic disease in dialysis patients is its own story — **another queued record, upstream and downstream at once**, because the treatment for this cancer damages the kidney too."
