    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**THE TREATMENT IS RADIATION TO THE BASE OF THE SKULL, AND EVERYTHING WITHIN A FEW CENTIMETRES OF THE TUMOUR IS SOMETHING YOU WOULD RATHER KEEP.** Permanent **xerostomia** from the salivary glands, as in every head and neck record. **Sensorineural hearing loss** from cochlear dose, compounded by cisplatin — which is the same combination `hearing-loss` and `tuberculosis` document from the other side, and it lands here as a permanent iatrogenic deafness that this register counts twice and connects never (gaps.md #160). **Hypopituitarism**, because the pituitary sits in the field, requiring lifelong hormone replacement in a substantial minority. **Hypothyroidism.** **Trismus** and neck fibrosis. **Cranial neuropathies.** **Carotid stenosis** and consequent stroke a decade later. **And the one the field fears: radiation-induced temporal lobe necrosis** — irradiated brain that dies, presenting years afterwards with seizures or cognitive decline. It is uncommon and it is the reason conformal planning matters so much here. **The toll is `major` rather than `catastrophic`, and the contrast with `head-neck-cancer` is the point.** Nobody loses a larynx or a jaw, because surgery is not the primary treatment. What they lose is saliva, hearing and endocrine function — permanent, life-altering, and not disfiguring. **Two diseases in the same anatomy, and the reason their tolls differ is that one is operated on and one is not.**",
