   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`established`, with one honest qualification that runs through the whole record: **Epstein-Barr virus is necessary and nowhere near sufficient.** More than nine in ten adults worldwide carry EBV for life and essentially none of them develop this cancer. What distinguishes the people who do is a combination of three things, and the model that fits the epidemiology requires all of them: **The virus.** The tumours are clonal for EBV — a single infected cell expanded — so infection precedes and enables the malignancy rather than following it. The cells run a **latency II** programme expressing LMP1, LMP2 and EBNA1, of which **LMP1 is the transforming one**, driving NF-κB and behaving as a constitutively active receptor. **The genome.** Susceptibility maps consistently to **HLA class I alleles at chromosome 6p21**, which is a plausible mechanism rather than a bare association: the alleles that raise risk are ones that present EBV antigens less effectively. **The exposure.** Nitrosamine-containing preserved foods — **salted fish consumed during weaning and early childhood** — carry a consistent and dose-related association across endemic populations, alongside tobacco. The childhood timing is what makes the falling incidence in Hong Kong and Singapore interpretable as a dietary effect with a forty-year lag. **The histology tracks the aetiology.** The undifferentiated non-keratinising type is the EBV-associated one and dominates in endemic areas; the keratinising squamous type behaves more like the rest of `head-neck-cancer`, is tobacco-related, and is what turns up in low-incidence regions. **What is not explained is the geography of the geography** — why the Maghreb and Inuit populations, on the far side of the world from southern China, share an elevated incidence that neither ancestry nor diet accounts for on its own.",
    "contested": "**WHETHER TO SCREEN, AND IT IS A REAL ARGUMENT RATHER THAN INERTIA.** The case for is the strongest early-detection evidence in this corpus: a cheap blood test, a stage shift from 5-7% to 47% stage I, and a disease where stage decides whether somebody keeps a cranial nerve. **The case against is methodological and this register is unusually well-placed to state it.** The comparison was against a historical cohort. **Stage shift, lead time and overdiagnosis are indistinguishable without a randomised mortality endpoint**, and `prostate-cancer` and `thyroid-cancer` are in this corpus specifically because a screening programme that produced a beautiful stage shift went on to harm people. The counter-argument to *that* is that nasopharyngeal carcinoma has no indolent reservoir — there is no latent pool of harmless tumours to overdiagnose the way there is in prostate and thyroid — so the analogy may simply not hold. **A second, narrower dispute: which test.** Plasma EBV DNA against the newer anti-BNLF2b antibody assay, with performance claims that have not been compared head to head at scale. **And a third that is really about money and geography.** Screening is plausibly cost-effective at Guangdong incidence and clearly is not at European incidence, so the question *should we screen* has different correct answers in different countries — which is gaps.md #52 turning up as a contested boundary rather than as a delivery gap. **The register takes no position and records that the dispute is between people who have read the same paper.**",
    "intervention": "`curative`, and this is the most curable disease in the head and neck by a wide margin. **Radiotherapy is the treatment and surgery is not.** The nasopharynx is at the skull base, behind the nose, adjacent to the carotid and the cranial nerves; it is not a site an operation reaches without unacceptable cost. It happens that the tumour is **exceptionally radiosensitive**, so the anatomy and the biology point the same way. **Intensity-modulated radiotherapy is standard** and matters more here than anywhere else because of what surrounds the target. **Chemotherapy is added by stage.** Concurrent cisplatin with radiotherapy for locally advanced disease since 1998; **induction gemcitabine and cisplatin** before it since around 2019. Adjuvant chemotherapy is increasingly selected by post-treatment plasma EBV DNA rather than given to everybody. **Recurrence has real options**, unusually: re-irradiation, and **salvage endoscopic nasopharyngectomy**, which reaches through the nose and is the one place surgery does contribute. Both carry heavier tolls than first treatment. **Metastatic disease** gets platinum-gemcitabine with a PD-1 inhibitor. **What does not exist is a treatment aimed at the virus.** EBV is present in every tumour cell, is clonal, and expresses viral proteins — which sounds like an ideal target and has produced no approved therapy. EBV-specific T cells and lytic-induction strategies remain experimental after two decades.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`, and the honest summary is that **the incidence fell without anybody intervening.** Hong Kong and Singapore have recorded substantial declines over recent decades, attributed principally to **less salted fish in early childhood** as diets changed with prosperity, alongside falling tobacco use. Nobody ran a programme. There is no attributable actor, no budget line, and no evaluation — it is the largest prevention effect in this record and it is invisible to every field in the schema (gaps.md #87). **AND THE VACCINE THAT SHOULD EXIST DOES NOT.** This register holds three virally-caused cancers. HPV causes `cervical-cancer` and part of `head-neck-cancer`, and there is a vaccine that prevents them. Hepatitis B causes much of `liver-cancer`, and there is a vaccine that prevents it — `liver-cancer`'s 1984 entry records the first demonstration anywhere that a vaccine could prevent a human cancer. **EBV causes this, and there is no licensed vaccine against EBV at all.** That is not for want of a target so much as for want of a market and a tractable immunogen: EBV is a large herpesvirus that establishes lifelong latency in essentially everybody, so a prophylactic vaccine has to prevent or control an infection that is close to universal — a much harder ask than HPV or HBV. Candidates against the gp350 envelope protein have existed for years without a licensed product. **An EBV vaccine would also touch infectious mononucleosis, several lymphomas, and — per `multiple-sclerosis`'s 2022 mechanism entry — possibly multiple sclerosis**, which makes it one of the highest-leverage missing interventions anywhere in this corpus.",
    "ongoing": "`moderate`, and it is the ongoing burden of what treatment took rather than of the disease. Surveillance, including **serial plasma EBV DNA**, which is one of the few follow-up tests in oncology that genuinely changes management. Lifelong thyroid function monitoring and replacement in many; pituitary hormone replacement in a minority. Dental care against radiation caries, saliva substitutes, hearing aids — **which is `hearing-loss`'s record arriving as a consequence of this one's treatment.** Swallowing therapy and management of neck fibrosis and trismus. The population is younger than most cancer populations — median age around the fifth decade in endemic areas — so **survivors live with all of this for thirty or forty years**, which is the same argument that drives the de-escalation dispute in `head-neck-cancer` and has produced less movement here."
   },
