    "deaths": {
     "value": 1500000.0,
     "units": "deaths/year from cirrhosis and chronic liver disease — around one in forty deaths worldwide, and rising in most high-income countries while most other causes fall",
     "src": "recall"
    },
    "note": "**THE CAUSES HAVE CHANGED COMPLETELY WITHIN ONE GENERATION AND THE ENTITY HAS NOT.** Cirrhosis was a viral hepatitis disease and an alcohol disease. **Hepatitis C became curable in 2014 and hepatitis B has been vaccine-preventable since 1982**, so the viral share is falling wherever those two records are delivered — and rising where they are not. **Alcohol-related liver disease has risen sharply**, particularly in younger adults and in women, with a marked acceleration around the pandemic years. And **metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease is the fastest-growing cause anywhere**, tracking `type-2-diabetes` and obesity, and is on course to become the leading indication for liver transplantation. So every blocker in this file would have been a different blocker in 1995, and the register stores one dated snapshot with no way to say the mix is moving under it. The nomenclature moved too: **NAFLD became MASLD and NASH became MASH in 2023**, a deliberate renaming to remove a term defined by absence and a term patients found stigmatising — which is gaps.md #133's lever, aimed at `#110`'s problem."
