     "kind": "policy",
     "blocks": "eligibility for a life-saving operation being conditioned on conduct",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THIS IS gaps.md #110's FLAGSHIP AND IT IS WORSE THAN THAT GAP ASSUMED.** funding per death, lower screening uptake, and being asked whether you smoked. **Here the blame is operational.** It is a written eligibility criterion, applied to a named individual, with a mortality consequence that has been measured in a trial. The six-month abstinence rule governed liver transplantation for alcohol-related disease for decades. Its stated justification was prognostic — that abstinence predicts post-transplant relapse. **The evidence for that claim is weak**, and severe alcohol-associated hepatitis kills roughly three quarters of patients inside the waiting period, so the rule's practical effect was to exclude most candidates by killing them. Early transplantation without the waiting period produces around 94% six-month survival against about 11% with medical management, with return to drinking in a minority comparable to other indications. Practice has shifted and is uneven, and fixed abstinence periods persist in many centres. **The structure worth naming: a rule justified on clinical grounds and functioning on moral ones is only falsifiable on the clinical claim, so refuting the clinical claim does not remove the rule.** That is why this moved slowly after 2019 and why it has not moved everywhere. The same shape sits elsewhere in medicine — smoking cessation before lung transplantation, BMI and smoking thresholds for elective surgery, and the sobriety requirements United States Medicaid programmes attached to hepatitis C antivirals until they were litigated away. **Rated `documented`: the criteria are published, the trial results are published, and the variation between centres is a matter of record.** The register takes no position on whether the judgement is right; it records that it is made and what it costs.",
