     "kind": "logistics",
     "blocks": "organs, and the capacity to use them",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "Liver transplantation is limited by donor organs, which cannot be manufactured (gaps.md #128), and by everything around them: retrieval teams able to operate at any hour, transplant surgical capacity, intensive care beds, and lifelong follow-up. **The supply side is genuinely movable and is moving.** Opt-out consent legislation, living donor liver transplantation — routine in parts of Asia and uncommon in the West — split-liver grafts, donation after circulatory death, and **normothermic machine perfusion**, which keeps a liver functioning outside the body and allows marginal organs to be assessed and used rather than discarded. That last one has measurably increased usable organs where adopted. **The demand side is about to get much worse.** Metabolic liver disease is on course to become the leading indication, in a population that is older and has more cardiovascular and renal comorbidity — which makes them harder to transplant and worse candidates on every axis. And in most of the world liver transplantation is simply unavailable, which makes the compensated-stratum interventions above the only realistic answer for most of this record's patients.",
