   "window": {
    "what": "**TREATED EARLY THIS IS A SURVIVABLE DISEASE, AND TREATED LATE IT IS NOT.** Viral load at presentation is the strongest predictor of death, and the monoclonals work dramatically better in patients who arrive before it climbs. In the trials that licensed them, patients presenting with low viral load had mortality in the low tens of percent or better; those presenting late died at rates not far off the untreated figure. So the intervention is excellent and the arrival is the problem, and the reasons people arrive late are not medical. The early symptoms look like malaria. The treatment unit is where people are known to die, and in early outbreaks going there means dying without your family. Transport is hard, and in Kivu the response itself was attacked. **THERE IS A SECOND WINDOW AND IT IS THE MORE IMPORTANT ONE.** Not the time from symptom to treatment for one patient, but the time from the index case to recognition of the outbreak. West Africa 2014 went roughly three months unrecognised and produced 28,600 cases. Outbreaks detected within weeks have produced dozens. The schema holds the first window and not the second. `caught_in_time` below is per patient; the number that actually decides the death toll is per outbreak, and there is no field for it (gaps.md #99).",
    "closes_on": "rising viral load and progression to hypovolaemic shock and multi-organ failure — days, and earlier is better continuously rather than at a threshold",
    "caught_in_time": {
     "value": 0.3,
     "units": "fraction-of-cases (reaching a treatment centre early enough for monoclonals to give their full benefit) — a reasoned estimate across recent outbreaks, and it varies enormously between them",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
