   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "clinical",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "none",
    "witness": "A measurement gap that is pure logistics rather than science. Antivenoms are species-specific and the biting snake is usually unidentified, so the right product is chosen by syndrome and geography and may simply not work. A cheap point-of-care venom-detection dipstick would raise efficacy without any new therapeutic — one of the highest-leverage and least glamorous measurement problems in the register."
   },
   "toll": {
    "severity": "minor",
    "permanent": false,
    "what": "Antivenom is heterologous animal protein: early anaphylactic reactions occur in a substantial minority and serum sickness later, both usually manageable where staff and adrenaline are present — which is not everywhere. The permanent disability in this record comes from the VENOM and from delay, not from the treatment, which is exactly why the access blocker is the whole story.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.05,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated (serious reaction to antivenom)",
     "src": "recall"
    }
   },
   "residue": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "Antivenom neutralises circulating venom. It does not reverse tissue that the venom has already digested, which is why the delay between bite and treatment decides the limb even when it does not decide the life. Cytotoxic envenoming produces necrosis, compartment syndrome, secondary infection, contracture and amputation; survivors are frequently left unable to do the agricultural work they were doing when bitten, in populations with no disability support and no alternative livelihood. This is the record where the residue plainly exceeds the mortality in total burden and is counted even less well. Deaths are estimated; permanent disability after survival is barely estimated at all.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.2,
     "units": "fraction-of-survivors (permanent physical disability — amputation, contracture, chronic ulceration, or lasting loss of function)",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "Venom toxinology is well characterised for the medically important species: haemotoxic, neurotoxic and cytotoxic components are identified down to individual toxin families, and the pathophysiology of coagulopathy, neuromuscular blockade and local necrosis follows from them. Antivenom works by direct neutralisation — as mechanistically clean as medicine gets.",
    "intervention": "`curative`. Adequate antivenom given in time reverses envenoming and the patient goes home; the disease does not persist or recur. The technology is from the 1890s — hyperimmunised animal plasma, essentially unchanged in principle.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`. Footwear, torches, protective agricultural practice and bed nets all help; none is prophylaxis.",
    "ongoing": "`low` for survivors of systemic envenoming, but the amputations and contractures are permanent and heavily concentrated in young agricultural workers, so the disability burden substantially exceeds the death count.",
    "access": "The whole story. Antivenom is expensive relative to rural incomes, requires cold chain, must be given in a facility able to manage anaphylaxis, and is time-critical. Some products marketed in Africa have been shown to be ineffective against local species — an active harm, since a family may sell assets to buy a vial that cannot work."
   },
