    "note": "**EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE IS AN AVERAGE OVER SIXTY DISEASES.** That is stated once here rather than repeated in every field. Incidence rose through the second half of the twentieth century in high-income countries and has broadly plateaued. Part of the rise was real — HIV-associated lymphoma, an ageing population, immunosuppression after transplantation — and part was reclassification, as entities were split, renamed and moved in and out of the category by successive WHO revisions. **The category itself has changed shape several times**, which makes any long-run trend partly a measurement of nomenclature. Several constituent diseases have identified infectious causes, and the register already holds three of them: *Helicobacter pylori* for gastric MALT, HIV for the AIDS-associated lymphomas, and hepatitis C for a share of marginal zone disease. Epstein-Barr virus, which drives endemic Burkitt lymphoma alongside malaria in equatorial Africa, is not a record here and probably should be."
