   "residue": {
    "severity": "minor",
    "permanent": false,
    "what": "**Rated low because the harm is not established, not because it is absent.** Persistent urethritis in men is well documented and resolves with effective treatment. The consequences that would matter — pelvic inflammatory disease, tubal infertility, preterm birth — are associated with this organism in some studies and not others, with substantial confounding by coexisting chlamydia and by sexual-behaviour variables. **`genital-chlamydia` and `gonorrhoea` both carry `major` permanent residue for the same anatomy on much stronger evidence.** This record deliberately does not, and the difference is the evidence rather than the biology. If the reproductive associations turn out to be causal, this rating is badly wrong and the natural-history blocker below is why nobody can say.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.02,
     "units": "fraction-of-infected left with lasting consequence — reasoned and conservative, pending the natural-history question",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`partial`, and the split is unusual: **the organism is well characterised and what it does to people is not.** *M. genitalium* — reclassified by some taxonomies into genus *Mycoplasmoides* — has the smallest genome of any self-replicating free-living bacterium at around 580 kilobases, which is why it became the template for the minimal-genome and synthetic-cell work. It has **no cell wall**, so every beta-lactam is useless against it, and it adheres to and damages urogenital epithelium with a terminal organelle. It is among the slowest-growing bacteria known and is effectively unculturable in routine practice, which is why it went thirty years between identification and a usable diagnostic. **What is not established is the natural history**: what fraction of infections cause disease, whether it causes the reproductive harms attributed to it, and whether an asymptomatic infection is worth finding.",
    "intervention": "`curative` where the right drug is chosen, and the qualifier carries the record. Doxycycline first to reduce organism load, then azithromycin if the strain is macrolide-susceptible or moxifloxacin if it is not. **With resistance testing, cure exceeds ninety percent; without it, blind azithromycin cures perhaps forty**, because most circulating strains are already resistant. **The options were narrow from the start and are narrowing.** No cell wall means no beta-lactams. Macrolide resistance is above fifty percent across much of the high-income world; fluoroquinolone resistance is rising fastest in the Asia-Pacific; and pristinamycin is a salvage agent most countries cannot obtain. **Genuinely untreatable cases are reported.**",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`. Condoms, partner treatment, and **explicitly not screening asymptomatic people** — which is the recommendation of every major guideline and is worth stating as a prevention position rather than an omission. The reasoning is unlike anything else here: finding an asymptomatic infection leads to treating it; treating it selects resistance; and because the prognostic question is unanswered, nobody can say the infection was going to harm the person. **The intervention's cost falls on future patients and its benefit is unproven for the present one.** There is no vaccine and no serious programme toward one.",
    "ongoing": "`none`. A course of antibiotics, and either it works or a different course follows. **The burden this record imposes is on the antibiotic supply rather than on the patient.**",
    "efficacy": "See the axis, and read it as a description of health systems rather than of pharmacology. The same organism is cured nine times in ten in Melbourne and four times in ten where resistance testing is unavailable.",
    "access": "**The test is four years old in most markets and the resistance test is rarer.** That is the whole of the access problem: the drugs are generic and stocked, and choosing between them requires information most clinicians cannot obtain. A patient with persistent urethritis in a system without resistance testing receives sequential empirical courses — which is the behaviour that produced the resistance in the first place."
   },
