     "blocks": "knowing whether this infection matters, which decides everything else",
     "what": "**THE FIELD CAN CHOOSE THE RIGHT ANTIBIOTIC FOR AN INFECTION IT CANNOT SAY NEEDED TREATING.** Urethritis in men is a solid association. Cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, preterm birth and tubal infertility are associated in some studies and not others, confounded by coexisting chlamydia and by behaviour, and a substantial share of infection appears to clear spontaneously without causing anything. **Every other decision in this record is downstream of that gap.** Whether to screen asymptomatic people; whether the current guideline advice not to is protecting the antibiotic supply or leaving women with preventable tubal damage; how much to invest in resistance testing; whether a second drug class is urgent. All of it turns on a natural-history question that requires long cohorts with reproductive endpoints, and those have not been done. **`genital-chlamydia` has the same question and forty years of data.** This record has the same question and four. `scale: null` for the biology; the cohort study is priceable and nobody is running it.",
