     "blocks": "finding the carriers, and imaging the patients",
     "what": "Two failures at opposite ends of the same life cycle. THE CARRIERS ARE INVISIBLE. An adult tapeworm produces almost no symptoms, so nobody presents; stool microscopy is insensitive and cannot distinguish the pork tapeworm from the beef tapeworm, which matters because only one causes cysticercosis. Coproantigen and molecular tests do better and are not routine anywhere the disease is. So the entire egg source of the disease goes undetected, and a single dose of praziquantel would clear it. THE PATIENTS NEED A SCANNER. Neurocysticercosis is diagnosed on CT or MRI, which is decisive and is in referral hospitals, and the disease is in villages that keep pigs. Serology helps and does not substitute. The register's cheapest-lever claim holds at the first end and not the second: finding carriers is a test-and-treat problem, and imaging is capital equipment.",
