     "what": "Interrupting this cycle requires treating human carriers, keeping pigs away from human faeces, treating and vaccinating pigs, and inspecting meat. Drop one and it re-establishes. Those four sit in health, in water and sanitation, in agriculture and veterinary services, and in food safety regulation respectively. The disease appears in the health budget. **There is a working pig vaccine and an effective porcine anthelmintic**, and buying them is an agriculture decision justified by a health outcome, which is the same structural problem echinococcosis and rabies have and this record makes three. Note the incentive: a smallholder gains little from vaccinating a pig against a parasite that does not make the pig ill and only costs money if the carcass is condemned. Meat inspection is what makes it worth their while, and meat inspection is exactly what does not exist in informal slaughter.",
