   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "good",
    "witness": "DIAGNOSTIC is `objective` — culture, susceptibility testing, and molecular detection of the carbapenemase gene. PROGNOSTIC is `partial`. Source, severity score, time to effective therapy and host state inform outcome; most CRE patients are already critically ill of something else, and separating what the organism did from what the underlying illness did is the recurring difficulty. **PREDICTIVE IS `good`, AND IT IS DOING MORE WORK HERE THAN IN ANY OTHER RECORD IN THIS REGISTER.** Identifying *which* carbapenemase the organism carries does not merely refine the choice of drug — it *determines* it, and the wrong choice is inactive rather than suboptimal. A serine carbapenemase is beaten by ceftazidime-avibactam; a metallo-beta-lactamase destroys the same combination and needs cefiderocol or aztreonam-avibactam. **One molecular result separates a drug that works from a drug that does nothing.** **AND THIS IS THE THIRD RECORD RUNNING WHERE THE SAME SEVENTY-YEAR-OLD TEST GETS A DIFFERENT RATING.** Typhoid `partial`, MRSA `good`, CRE `good` — and the differences are laboratories, not information. The convention this register has settled on is to rate the test's quality on `measurement` and put its availability in `access`, which is why this record's access figure is a quarter (gaps.md #142)."
   },
   "toll": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**THE TOLL IN THIS RECORD IS A DRUG THAT MEDICINE HAD ALREADY ABANDONED ONCE AND WENT BACK FOR.** **Colistin** — polymyxin E — was introduced in the 1950s and largely withdrawn in the 1970s because it was too nephrotoxic to justify when safer agents existed. It returned in the 2000s as a last-line agent against carbapenem-resistant Gram-negatives, unchanged, for the same organisms, at the same cost to the kidney. **Acute kidney injury occurs in something like a third to a half of treated patients**, a share of whom do not fully recover; neurotoxicity is common at higher doses. The other salvage agents are not much better. **Aminoglycosides** are nephrotoxic and cause **permanent sensorineural hearing loss**. **Tigecycline** was associated with higher all-cause mortality than comparators across its trial programme, which is a strange thing to say about a treatment. **AND THE TOLL FELL SHARPLY WHERE THE NEW AGENTS ARRIVED.** Ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam, imipenem-relebactam and cefiderocol are ordinary, well-tolerated beta-lactams. Trials that compared them against colistin-based regimens found both better survival and substantially less renal failure. **This record is rated `major` rather than `minor` because most of the world's CRE is still treated the old way.** Which raises the thing the schema cannot say: colistin's toxicity never changed. What changed is what it was being compared against (gaps.md #148).",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.2,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated (permanent harm — chiefly residual renal impairment after colistin and aminoglycoside hearing loss) — a reasoned estimate weighted toward the colistin-era regimens that remain the global default",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "window": {
    "what": "**THE WINDOW IS SHORTER THAN MRSA'S AND THE DEFAULT GUESS IS ALWAYS WRONG.** Empirical therapy for a septic patient is a carbapenem or something narrower. For CRE that is, by definition, inactive. So unlike MRSA — where empirical vancomycin at least sometimes covers the organism — **a patient with CRE is receiving nothing that works from the moment treatment starts until the laboratory says otherwise**, which is two to three days by conventional culture. Time to effective therapy in carbapenem-resistant Gram-negative bacteraemia is one of the better-established predictors of mortality in infectious disease, and the delay here is close to guaranteed. Rapid molecular panels run directly off a positive blood culture bottle and return the carbapenemase gene in about an hour. They exist, they work, and they cost more per test than the culture they sit on top of.",
    "closes_on": "progression of inadequately treated Gram-negative bacteraemia to septic shock and multi-organ failure — hours to days",
    "caught_in_time": {
     "value": 0.35,
     "units": "fraction-of-invasive-cases (receiving an active agent promptly) — a reasoned estimate, and the lowest in the bacterial block for the reason above",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
