   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "partial",
    "witness": "DIAGNOSTIC is `objective` — endoscopy with biopsy — and as in colorectal cancer **the diagnostic instrument is also the curative one** for early disease: a lesion confined to the mucosa can be removed by endoscopic submucosal dissection in the same session, and the patient keeps their stomach. The obstacle is that early gastric cancer produces no symptoms. Dyspepsia is universal and non-specific; by the time there is weight loss, vomiting or anaemia the disease is usually advanced. **Which is why the countries that do well are the ones that scope people who feel fine.** PROGNOSTIC is `partial` and stage does nearly all of it. Depth of invasion — mucosa, submucosa, muscularis — decides whether the cure is an endoscopy or an operation, and it is assessed by the endoscopist before anything is removed. PREDICTIVE is `partial` and improving from a low base. **HER2 amplification** in roughly a fifth selects trastuzumab. **Mismatch-repair-deficient and EBV-associated tumours** respond to checkpoint blockade far better than the rest, which is the same two-percent-of-the-record story colorectal cancer tells. **Claudin 18.2** is a recent addition with its own antibody. PD-L1 shifts the odds without deciding. That leaves the majority — chromosomally unstable, HER2-negative, microsatellite-stable disease — with chemotherapy chosen by tolerability."
   },
   "toll": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**LOSING YOUR STOMACH CHANGES EVERY MEAL FOR THE REST OF YOUR LIFE, AND THE REGISTER HAS NO BETTER WORD FOR IT THAN `major`.** After a total gastrectomy a person eats small amounts many times a day and cannot do otherwise. **Dumping syndrome** — food entering the small bowel undiluted — causes sweating, palpitations, cramping and diarrhoea after eating, and reactive hypoglycaemia afterwards. **Vitamin B12 must be injected for life**, because the intrinsic factor that absorbed it was in the removed stomach. Iron deficiency, calcium and vitamin D malabsorption, osteoporosis, and sustained weight loss that frequently never fully reverses. Subtotal resection is better and not normal. Perioperative chemotherapy adds neuropathy. D2 lymphadenectomy adds surgical morbidity. **AND THE MOST DAMAGING PROCEDURE IN THIS RECORD IS GIVEN TO PEOPLE WHO DO NOT HAVE CANCER.** Carriers of a pathogenic *CDH1* variant — hereditary diffuse gastric cancer — face a lifetime risk in the region of seventy percent, arising in a mucosa that endoscopy cannot reliably survey. The standard recommendation is **prophylactic total gastrectomy, often in a person's twenties or thirties.** A certain, permanent, life-altering harm to prevent a probable cancer, in someone who is well. That is gaps.md #105 — `toll` is scoped to treatment and prevention has costs — in its sharpest form anywhere in the corpus.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.5,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated (permanent harm — gastrectomy and its nutritional consequences, chemotherapy neuropathy) — a reasoned estimate, and it EXCLUDES the early-stage patients cured endoscopically, which is the point of finding them early",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "window": {
    "what": "**THE WINDOW BUYS YOU YOUR STOMACH AS WELL AS YOUR LIFE, WHICH IS UNUSUAL.** Gastric cancer confined to the mucosa is cured in over ninety percent of cases — **by endoscopic submucosal dissection, without surgery, with the stomach intact.** Once it invades the submucosa and beyond, the answer is gastrectomy, and past that it is not curable at all. So the difference between finding it early and finding it late is the difference between an outpatient procedure and eating six small meals a day for the rest of your life. `window_toll_link` fires on this record and reports that **the toll is the price of missing it and diagnosis is the cheap lever** — which for prostate cancer was exactly backwards and here is exactly right. **AND TWO COUNTRIES HAVE DEMONSTRATED IT AT NATIONAL SCALE.** Japan has screened for gastric cancer since the 1960s and Korea since 1999, both now principally by endoscopy, and both detect a large share of their cancers at the endoscopically curable stage. Their survival figures are roughly double everybody else's. The upstream window is longer still: the Correa cascade from chronic gastritis through atrophy and intestinal metaplasia takes decades, and eradicating the infection partway along reduces the risk.",
    "closes_on": "invasion beyond the mucosa — at which point the cure becomes a gastrectomy — and then beyond the stomach wall and to nodes",
    "caught_in_time": {
     "value": 0.3,
     "units": "fraction-of-cases (diagnosed at a stage curable by endoscopic resection or by surgery with good expectation) — a reasoned global estimate. **In Japan and Korea it is far higher and everywhere else far lower**, which is the whole record",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
