   "moved": [
    {
     "date": 1994,
     "axis": "prevention",
     "from": "none",
     "to": "prophylaxis",
     "why": "**HELICOBACTER PYLORI WAS CLASSIFIED AS A GROUP 1 HUMAN CARCINOGEN**, a decade after it was shown to cause peptic ulcer disease, and gastric cancer acquired a preventable cause for the first time. Eradication reduces subsequent gastric cancer incidence, most clearly in people treated before atrophy and intestinal metaplasia are established. **Japan approved eradication for all infected people in 2013 explicitly for cancer prevention** and has pursued it as national policy since. The prevention of this cancer is two weeks of antibiotics for a different disease, which this register already holds as `h-pylori-ulcer`.",
     "src": {
      "id": "PMID:7715068",
      "retrieved": "2026-08-26",
      "quote": "Schistosomes, liver flukes and Helicobacter pylori."
     }
    },
    {
     "date": 2000,
     "axis": "toll",
     "from": 0.75,
     "to": 0.5,
     "why": "**ENDOSCOPIC SUBMUCOSAL DISSECTION**, developed in Japan through the 1990s and spreading from around 2000, made mucosal gastric cancer curable without an operation. The patient keeps their stomach. Capability did not change — those patients were curable by gastrectomy before. **What collapsed is the price**, from a life reorganised around eating to an outpatient procedure and a surveillance endoscopy. The numbers above are the toll's *incidence* rather than its severity, and `moved` records a change on an axis rather than in one of its components — the same limitation prostate cancer's 2012 entry ran into (gaps.md #26)."
    }
   ],
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`established`, and it is one of the few cancers with a named bacterial cause and a stepwise histological path that can be watched through an endoscope. **The Correa cascade**: *Helicobacter pylori* colonises the gastric mucosa and produces chronic active gastritis; over decades this becomes atrophic gastritis, then intestinal metaplasia, then dysplasia, then carcinoma. Salt and salt-preserved food accelerate it; smoking contributes; the bacterium's own virulence factors — *cagA* above all — determine much of the risk. That accounts for intestinal-type, non-cardia disease, which is most of the global burden and the part that is falling. **The molecular classification splits it four ways** — Epstein-Barr-positive, microsatellite-unstable, genomically stable diffuse, and chromosomally unstable — and those groups differ in age, prognosis and what they respond to, which is why the `strata` block above is written the way it is. **DIFFUSE-TYPE DISEASE IS A DIFFERENT PROBLEM.** Signet-ring cell carcinoma infiltrates the stomach wall without forming a mass, strikes younger patients, is less clearly *H. pylori*-dependent, and is the form inherited with *CDH1* variants. It is not declining. **THE NAMED HOLE IS WHO.** Roughly half the world carries *H. pylori* and one to two percent of the infected develop gastric cancer. Bacterial virulence factors, host inflammatory genotype, salt intake and the timing of acquisition all contribute and none of it identifies an individual — which is the same question COPD asks about smokers, and it is what makes mass eradication a blunt instrument.",
    "intervention": "`curative`, and this record has two cures that are barely the same kind of thing. **Endoscopic submucosal dissection** removes a mucosal cancer through the mouth, cures over ninety percent of them, and leaves the stomach working. **Gastrectomy** removes the organ, cures a substantial minority of locally advanced disease with perioperative chemotherapy, and reorganises the rest of the patient's life around eating. For advanced disease: chemotherapy, with trastuzumab for HER2-positive tumours, checkpoint blockade for the mismatch-repair-deficient and EBV-associated minority, and an anti-claudin antibody more recently. Median survival around a year, and the last fifteen years reached about a tenth of the record.",
    "prevention": "`prophylaxis`, **and the prophylaxis is antibiotics for somebody else's disease.** Eradicating *Helicobacter pylori* reduces subsequent gastric cancer, particularly when done before atrophy is established. Japan has treated it as national cancer-prevention policy since 2013; most countries have not, and the reason is in the blockers below. Then salt reduction, smoking cessation, and — historically and by far the largest of them — **the change in how food is preserved.** **THE GREAT DECLINE WAS NOT A MEDICAL ACHIEVEMENT.** Gastric cancer incidence has been falling across the developed world since roughly the 1930s, decades before *H. pylori* was discovered, and the leading explanation is domestic refrigeration replacing salting and smoking, together with falling *H. pylori* transmission as sanitation improved and households became less crowded. **Nobody did any of that to prevent cancer.** It is plausibly the largest cancer prevention in human history, it has no programme and no ministry, and this schema can only record deliberate acts. See gaps.md #129.",
    "ongoing": "`moderate`, and it is carrying two very different populations. A patient cured endoscopically carries surveillance endoscopy and the knowledge that the rest of the stomach is still metaplastic. A patient after total gastrectomy carries **lifelong vitamin B12 injections, iron and calcium supplementation, bone density monitoring, dietetic support, and a permanently altered relationship with food.** The axis records that as the same word."
   },
   "holes": [
    "Every scalar is `src: recall` or `src: reasoning`, and the strata fractions are global averages over a bimodal world — in Japan and Korea the early-stage fraction is far larger and in most countries far smaller.",
    "Cardia and non-cardia disease are counted together here and are diverging in opposite directions with different causes. They may deserve separate records.",
    "The magnitude of the refrigeration effect is an inference from time trends and ecological comparison rather than a measured quantity, and it is stated as the leading explanation rather than a settled one.",
    "The benefit of mass *H. pylori* eradication in preventing cancer is established in trials in high-incidence populations; whether it transfers to low-incidence populations, and at what cost in resistance, is unresolved.",
    "Oesophageal cancer shares the cardia boundary and is absent from this register. So is `hepatitis-b`, `cirrhosis` and now a fourth upstream entity — the queue keeps producing records whose causes are not yet written."
   ],
