     "kind": "logistics",
     "blocks": "endoscopy for people who feel perfectly well",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**TWO COUNTRIES HAVE PROVED IT WORKS AND ALMOST NOBODY ELSE DOES IT.** Japan and Korea screen at national scale, find a large share of their cancers while they are mucosal, cure them endoscopically, and report roughly double everybody else's survival. The intervention is an endoscopy in a person with no symptoms. Elsewhere the arithmetic does not obviously work: at low incidence, screening a population by endoscopy costs more per cancer found than anybody will pay, and the procedure has its own small risk. **So the strategy is correct, demonstrated, and rationally declined by most of the world** — which is a different kind of blocker from the ones this register usually records. The constraint is the same one colorectal cancer names: endoscopists and rooms, made slowly, not shippable. Where both cancers are common the capacity serves both, which nothing here can express.",
