     "kind": "policy",
     "blocks": "deciding whether to give half the world antibiotics",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**ERADICATING *H. PYLORI* AT POPULATION SCALE MEANS TREATING ROUGHLY HALF OF HUMANITY TO PREVENT CANCER IN ONE OR TWO PERCENT OF THEM.** The benefit is real and is demonstrated in high-incidence populations. The costs are: a course of two or three antibiotics per person, side effects, the disruption of a microbiome that may not be entirely a parasite, and **antimicrobial resistance** — clarithromycin resistance in *H. pylori* is already rising and is the main reason first-line regimens fail. That last cost lands in `amr-infection`, which is a separate record in this register, and **nothing can say that a prevention for one entity worsens another** (gaps.md #130). Japan decided the trade was worth it and treats eradication as cancer policy. Most countries have not decided, and the undecided position is itself a choice being made every year. A vaccine against *H. pylori* would dissolve the whole problem and does not exist.",
