     "obstacles": [
      "who-progresses"
     ],
     "what": "Two absences and the first would settle the policy question above. **WHICH OF THE INFECTED WILL GET CANCER.** One to two percent, over decades, and the determinants — bacterial *cagA* status, host inflammatory genotype, salt intake, age at acquisition — stratify populations without identifying people. **A test that named the one-in-fifty would turn mass eradication into targeted eradication and remove the resistance argument entirely.** **AND DIFFUSE-TYPE DISEASE HAS NO ANSWER.** It infiltrates without forming a mass, so endoscopic screening is much less effective against it; it strikes younger people; it is not clearly falling; and for hereditary *CDH1* carriers the only reliable option is removing a healthy stomach. `scale: null`.",
