   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "partial",
    "witness": "**DIAGNOSTIC IS `objective` AND THE SCREEN COSTS ALMOST NOTHING.** Echocardiography defines the anatomy completely and non-invasively; there is no diagnostic uncertainty worth arguing about once a child reaches a machine and somebody who can read it. **The screen that matters is a pulse oximeter on a newborn's foot.** Critical congenital heart disease — the duct-dependent lesions that collapse when the ductus arteriosus closes in the first days of life — is detectable by measuring oxygen saturation before discharge. It was added to national newborn screening panels from 2011 and it is cheap, quick and effective. **And it carries the flaw this register flagged at `chronic-kidney-disease`: pulse oximetry accuracy varies with skin pigmentation** (gaps.md #175). In a screen whose entire job is detecting a few percentage points of desaturation in a newborn, that is not a theoretical concern, and it is a third record in which the register has recommended this device without qualifying the rating. **Prenatal detection is the other half and it is worse than people assume.** Fetal echocardiography can identify major structural disease, and detection rates for major lesions run between roughly a third and two thirds even in well-resourced systems, depending on who performs the anomaly scan and what views they are trained to obtain. PROGNOSTIC is `partial`: the anatomical diagnosis predicts a great deal, and within a lesion the range of outcomes is wide and not individually predictable. PREDICTIVE is `partial` — the anatomy determines the operation, which is a genuine prediction, and nothing predicts who will develop late ventricular failure or arrhythmia."
   },
   "toll": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**THE COST OF FIXING A HEART IN A NEWBORN IS PAID PARTLY IN THE BRAIN, AND IT IS UNDER-DISCUSSED.** Children who undergo cardiac surgery as neonates have measurably worse neurodevelopmental outcomes than their peers — executive function, attention, motor skills, academic attainment. The contributions are argued over: cardiopulmonary bypass and circulatory arrest, pre-operative hypoxia, prolonged intensive care, and abnormalities of brain development that are present *before* any operation in some lesions. **Neurodevelopmental surveillance is now a formal part of congenital cardiac care**, which is a field acknowledging a permanent cost of its own success. Then the ordinary surgical tolls, at scale: a sternotomy in infancy and often several more, arrhythmias arising from surgical scar, permanent pacemakers, prosthetic valves and the anticoagulation that goes with mechanical ones, and for many lesions **planned reoperation** — a repaired tetralogy of Fallot typically needs its pulmonary valve replaced in adult life. **And the imposition that has no field**: a childhood organised around a heart condition, the restrictions and the surveillance, and — for parents — a decision taken on a newborn's behalf under conditions of extreme urgency.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.2,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated (permanent harm — chiefly neurodevelopmental impairment after neonatal surgery, and surgical arrhythmia requiring a device) — a reasoned estimate, concentrated in the complex lesions",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "residue": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**THE FONTAN CIRCULATION IS THE CLEAREST EXAMPLE IN THIS REGISTER OF MEDICINE BUILDING SOMETHING THAT WORKS AND HAS A KNOWN EXPIRY.** A child born with one functional ventricle cannot be repaired, so surgeons **substitute a different circulation**: systemic venous blood is routed directly to the lungs without a pump, and the single ventricle serves the body. It works. Children who died in infancy now reach adulthood. **And it was understood from the start that it produces chronically elevated systemic venous pressure**, because passive pulmonary flow requires it. Three decades later the consequences are catalogued: **Fontan-associated liver disease**, in which fibrosis is close to universal with time and cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma occur; **protein-losing enteropathy**; **plastic bronchitis**; lymphatic failure; and progressive ventricular dysfunction ending in transplantation. **A cardiac operation produces a liver disease, predictably, on a thirty-year timetable** — and `cirrhosis` is a record in this register with no way to say where a share of it came from (gaps.md #147, #160). Beyond the Fontan: repaired lesions leave residual defects, scar-related arrhythmias, and in late-repaired shunts **irreversible pulmonary vascular disease** (see `window`). Almost nobody with complex congenital heart disease has a normal heart afterwards; they have a **different** one.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.25,
     "units": "fraction-of-survivors (permanent residual disease — arrhythmia, ventricular dysfunction, Fontan-associated organ failure, or the need for further surgery) — a reasoned estimate",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "window": {
    "what": "**THIS RECORD HAS THREE WINDOWS AND ONE OF THEM CLOSES BY MAKING THE OPERATION LETHAL** (gaps.md #199). *Days.* Duct-dependent lesions depend on the ductus arteriosus staying open; it closes in the first days of life and the infant collapses. **Prostaglandin E1 holds it open** — a cheap infusion that buys time for transfer — and the window between a well-looking newborn and a dying one is measured in hours. Pulse oximetry screening exists to catch exactly this. *Months.* Most repairs are timed to a lesion's natural history: too early and the child is small and the repair fragile, too late and damage accrues. ***Years, and then it inverts.*** A large unrepaired left-to-right shunt — a ventricular septal defect, an atrioventricular septal defect — sends excessive flow to the lungs, and the pulmonary arterioles remodel irreversibly. Once pulmonary vascular resistance exceeds systemic, the shunt **reverses**: this is **Eisenmenger syndrome**, and at that point **closing the defect is contraindicated and would kill the patient**, because the shunt has become the right ventricle's only escape from a circulation it can no longer drive. **The same operation, on the same anatomy, that would have cured this child at six months will kill them at five years.** The window did not expire. It inverted. Children with Down syndrome and atrioventricular septal defects develop this especially early, which is why timely repair in that group is a specific and frequently missed priority.",
    "closes_on": "ductal closure in the first days of life; and, for unrepaired shunts, irreversible pulmonary vascular remodelling, after which the corrective operation becomes fatal",
    "caught_in_time": {
     "value": 0.2,
     "units": "fraction-of-cases (reaching surgery while it is still both possible and corrective) — a reasoned estimate; it approaches one in countries with screening and surgery and near zero where neither exists",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
