     "blocks": "the adults that paediatric surgery created, and the service that does not exist for them",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THERE ARE NOW MORE ADULTS THAN CHILDREN LIVING WITH CONGENITAL HEART DISEASE IN SEVERAL COUNTRIES, AND THEY ARE A POPULATION NOBODY PLANNED FOR.** A repaired heart is not a normal heart. Adults with congenital heart disease develop arrhythmias from surgical scar, ventricular dysfunction, valve degeneration requiring reoperation, pulmonary hypertension, and — for Fontan patients — liver and lymphatic failure. Managing that requires specialists trained in an anatomy that adult cardiologists were never taught. **The documented failure is the transition.** A substantial share of patients are lost to specialist follow-up when they leave paediatric services in their late teens — the point at which they feel well, dislike hospitals, and are handed to a system that may not have a congenital service. They reappear years later with problems that were manageable and are no longer. **This is gaps.md #147 producing an administrative rather than a biological consequence**: capability created a cohort, and the cohort has no home. `cystic-fibrosis` and `spinal-muscular-atrophy` are generating the same problem now, a generation behind.",
