     "from": 0.15,
     "to": 0.22,
     "why": "**KRAS WAS UNDRUGGABLE FOR FORTY YEARS AND THIS FILE SAID SO IN THE PRESENT TENSE UNTIL 26 AUGUST 2026.** The FDA approved **daraxonrasib** (RASONQUE), a multi-selective inhibitor of the RAS GTPase family, for metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma after at least one prior systemic therapy, or in people who are not candidates for multiagent chemotherapy. **RAS is mutated in roughly nine of ten pancreatic adenocarcinomas**, it has been the defining target of this disease since the 1980s, and every previous attempt to drug it failed — which is why `mechanism: established` had produced so little here. **The effect size is the largest ever reported in this setting.** RASolute 302 (NCT06625320) randomised 500 patients 1:1 against physician's-choice chemotherapy: median overall survival **13.2 months against 6.7**, hazard ratio **0.40**; progression-free survival 7.2 against 3.6 months; objective response 30% against 11%. Second-line metastatic pancreatic cancer is a setting where trials have failed for thirty years and where a hazard ratio of 0.8 would have been publishable. **Three qualifications, because this is the record most likely to be over-read.** It is **second-line**, so it does not touch the fifth of patients who present resectable. **Nobody is cured** — median survival roughly doubles and the disease still kills almost everyone, which is why `intervention` does not move and the stratum stays `disease-modifying`. And the label carries dermatologic and soft tissue toxicity, stomatitis, diarrhoea, gastrointestinal perforation and interstitial lung disease. **AND THE ACCESS ARITHMETIC NOW RUNS BACKWARDS** (gaps.md #215). `efficacy` is defined as what the *best available* treatment achieves, so this entry raises it — and the best available treatment is a once-daily tablet approved in one country and priced like a new oncology drug. **Nobody lost anything and `access` should fall**, because the denominator moved underneath it. Recorded as a separate move when there is a number to put on it. This is the register's **first `moved` entry written the day after the thing happened.** Every other one is history. `moved:` exists to make a second pass a comparison rather than a fresh opinion, and that only works if somebody writes the entry while the change is still legible.",
