    "deaths": {
     "value": 35000.0,
     "units": "deaths/year — and the figure is now almost entirely a statement about who has access to a generic tablet, not about the disease",
     "src": "reasoning"
    },
    "note": "**THE PREVALENCE-INCIDENCE RELATIONSHIP HERE IS THE OPPOSITE OF EVERY OTHER RECORD IN THIS REGISTER**, and it is a direct consequence of the drug working. Before 2001, CML incidence and CML mortality were nearly the same number — people were diagnosed and died within a few years. Now incidence is unchanged and mortality has collapsed in treated populations, so the prevalent population accumulates. In high-income countries the number of people living with CML is projected to keep rising for decades before it stabilises. That is a success, and it is also the reason the drug's economics look the way they do: **a growing market with a fixed incidence, produced by efficacy.** See gaps.md #124. Where TKIs are unavailable or unaffordable, CML remains what it was in 1999. The disease is the same everywhere; the outcome is a function of whether a generic tablet and a molecular monitoring service exist."
