    "note": "**AGE-STANDARDISED PREVALENCE HAS RISEN, WHICH MEANS THIS IS NOT ONLY AGEING.** Populations getting older explains much of the growth and not all of it. The leading candidate for the remainder is **industrial and agricultural chemical exposure** — see the `policy` blocker — which would make Parkinson's substantially preventable and it is not treated as a preventable disease by anybody. Two of the most robust findings in its epidemiology run the other way and are unexplained: **smoking and caffeine consumption are both consistently associated with lower risk.** The register's most-blamed exposure appears to protect against this record, replicated across decades and populations, and nobody knows why."
