    "deaths": {
     "value": 175000.0,
     "units": "deaths/year — and the modelling that produced it estimates that **roughly 35,000 people a year die within twelve months of onset without ever being diagnosed**, mistaken for influenza or gastroenteritis until the ketoacidosis kills them",
     "src": "recall"
    },
    "note": "**THE MOST USEFUL NUMBER FOR THIS RECORD IS NOT A DEATH COUNT — IT IS THE MISSING PEOPLE.** Modelling published in 2022 estimated that about **3.9 million people were \"missing\" from the world's type 1 diabetes population**: alive if they had received the care available since 1922, and dead. It is a prevalence deficit rather than a mortality figure, and it captures something no field in this register does — **not how many died, but how many are not here.** The arithmetic behind it is the record's whole argument. Insulin is 103 years old. Its discoverers sold the patent for a dollar each. **A child diagnosed in Helsinki has a near-normal life expectancy and a child diagnosed in rural Mali frequently has months**, and the difference is not knowledge. Life expectancy loss runs from a few years in the best-served health systems to something like three decades in the worst."
