    {
     "kind": "diagnosis",
     "blocks": "two different things, and one of them kills people this week",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THE FIRST IS A GLUCOSE TEST THAT WAS NOT DONE.** Roughly 35,000 people a year die within a year of onset without ever being diagnosed, and about a third of children reach diagnosis already in ketoacidosis even in well-resourced systems. The presentation is unmistakable if considered and is mistaken for gastroenteritis or influenza when it is not. Awareness campaigns aimed at parents and primary care have measurably reduced DKA at presentation where they have been run, which makes this among the cheapest interventions anywhere in this register. **THE SECOND IS A SCREENING PROGRAMME THAT DOES NOT EXIST.** Islet autoantibody testing identifies preclinical disease years ahead, with roughly 85% progression at fifteen years for multiple antibodies. It is the entry ticket to teplizumab, and to the trial pipeline behind it. **So a licensed disease-modifying therapy has almost no eligible patients, because nobody is looking for them** (gaps.md #162 — but here the gate is cheap and simply is not run). General-population screening in children has been piloted and raises the ordinary questions about screening well people, with the unusual feature that the test is close to deterministic, so this is not an overdiagnosis problem. It is a diagnosis nobody is looking for.",
     "who_could": [
      "health-system",
      "government",
      "academic",
      "payer",
      "nonprofit"
     ],
     "scale": 1000000000.0,
     "standing": "documented",
     "src": "recall"
    },
    {
     "kind": "regulatory",
     "blocks": "a century-old biologic having no generic",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**INSULIN SPENT MOST OF A CENTURY WITHOUT A GENERIC, AND THE REASON IS CATEGORICAL RATHER THAN TECHNICAL.** Insulin is a biologic, and until recently it sat in a regulatory category that had no abbreviated approval pathway — small-molecule generics had one and biosimilars did not, so a molecule characterised in 1922 was treated as unrepeatable. Around that, manufacturers accumulated patents on **delivery devices, formulations and manufacturing processes** rather than on the molecule, which is enough to deter entrants without owning the thing itself. A 2020 change in United States law reclassified insulin as a biologic eligible for interchangeable biosimilar approval, and interchangeable products followed within a year — after decades in which none had been possible. **This blocker is one of only a handful in the register using `regulatory`**, and it earns it: the barrier was a classification, the fix was a statute, and nothing about the chemistry, the manufacturing or the clinical evidence changed. It is worth recording precisely because this register's default finding is that regulation is rarely the binding constraint — and here it demonstrably was, for a very long time, for the oldest biologic in medicine.",
     "who_could": [
      "regulator",
      "legislator",
      "government",
      "manufacturer"
     ],
     "scale": 500000000.0,
     "standing": "documented",
     "src": "recall"
    }
