   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "none",
    "witness": "**DIAGNOSTIC is `objective` and it answers the wrong question well.** IgG serology reliably tells you whether someone has ever been infected — and since a third of humanity has, a positive result is usually uninformative. What clinical decisions actually need is **when** infection occurred, and that is much harder: IgM persists for months to years and has poor specificity, so avidity testing exists precisely to date an infection that a single serology cannot. PCR on amniotic fluid establishes fetal infection; ocular disease is diagnosed by the appearance of the retina, because serology cannot distinguish the lesion in front of you from the two billion people who would also test positive. **A test that is 100% sensitive and nearly useless is a shape this register has not previously recorded.** PROGNOSTIC is `partial`. Gestational timing predicts congenital outcome well and in two opposite directions at once — transmission risk rises through pregnancy while severity falls — which is genuinely useful. What is not predicted is who among the infected will ever develop ocular disease, and that is most of the preventable harm. PREDICTIVE is `none`, and it drives the record's central controversy. **Nobody can say which pregnancy benefits from antenatal treatment.** The evidence that spiramycin reduces transmission, or that pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine reduces fetal damage, is weak enough that entire countries have reached opposite policy conclusions from the same literature — France screens every pregnancy, the United Kingdom and United States do not. See the `evidence-incomplete` blocker."
   },
