    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "partial",
    "witness": "**DIAGNOSTIC IS `objective` AND THE TEST HAS A PROPERTY NO OTHER OBJECTIVE TEST IN THIS REGISTER HAS: IT ONLY WORKS WHEN THE PATIENT IS UNWELL.** Asthma is defined by **variable** airflow obstruction, so the confirmatory tests measure variability — spirometry showing bronchodilator reversibility, peak flow diurnal variation, exhaled nitric oxide, or a bronchial challenge. All of them are normal between attacks, and **all of them are normal in a patient whose treatment is working.** The test that would confirm the diagnosis is abolished by treating the disease, and treatment is started before testing because the patient is breathless now. **The consequence is documented and it runs in both directions.** In wealthy systems, studies re-testing adults carrying a physician diagnosis of asthma find that **roughly a third do not have it** — a treatable disease diagnosed onto people who do not have it, who then carry the label, the inhaler and its toll for life. Everywhere else the diagnosis is missed or never made, because spirometry is not available and wheeze is treated as infection. It is `copd`'s complaint (gaps.md #90) with the mechanism inverted: there the threshold decides who has the disease; **here the timing does.** PROGNOSTIC is `partial` and asymmetric. Prior exacerbations predict future exacerbations better than anything else in respiratory medicine — the single most useful prognostic fact in the record. **What is not predicted is who dies**, because most asthma deaths occur in people classified as mild or moderate, which means the register's own severity gradings do not identify the people at risk of the outcome that matters most. And nothing predicts which child will still have asthma at thirty (gaps.md #205). PREDICTIVE is `partial`, and inside severe asthma it is genuinely **good** — one of the better biomarker stories in medicine. Blood eosinophil count, exhaled nitric oxide and IgE with allergen sensitisation select who responds to which biologic, and they are cheap and routine. **The rating is dragged down by everything outside that 6% of patients**: nothing predicts who will respond to inhaled corticosteroid, nothing predicts which of several biologics is right for a patient eligible for more than one, and there is no biomarker at all for the non-type-2 group, which is the group with nothing to offer."
