   "toll": {
    "severity": "minor",
    "permanent": false,
    "what": "**RATED FOR THE MODAL PATIENT AND THE MODAL PATIENT GETS OFF LIGHTLY, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THIS PERSISTS.** An unnecessary antibiotic course causes diarrhoea in roughly one patient in ten, plus rash, candidiasis and nausea — transient, unmemorable, and cheap enough individually that no prescriber ever sees a reason to stop. The tail is not trivial and is not rated here because it is rare: *Clostridioides difficile* colitis, which can be severe, recurrent and fatal; anaphylaxis; and **fluoroquinolone-associated tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy, which are permanent** and which regulators have warned about specifically in the context of self-limiting infections. **The honest rating is `minor` and the honest reading is that `minor` × three hundred million is not minor.** The register has no field for that multiplication and this record should not be inflated to compensate.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.12,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated experiencing an adverse drug effect — the common, transient kind; permanent harm is orders of magnitude rarer and is not what this figure counts",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`established`, and it is one of the least mysterious entries in the register. Acute bronchitis is inflammation of the large airways following infection — **rhinovirus, influenza, parainfluenza, RSV, coronaviruses and adenovirus account for the great majority**, with *Mycoplasma pneumoniae*, *Chlamydia pneumoniae* and *Bordetella pertussis* making up a small bacterial minority. The cough outlasts the infection because airway epithelium takes weeks to repair and remains transiently hyperresponsive, which is why the illness feels like it is not getting better long after the virus has gone. **Purulent sputum is the single most consequential misunderstanding in this record.** Green or yellow sputum is neutrophil myeloperoxidase — a marker of inflammation, not of bacteria — and both patients and clinicians read it as the indication for an antibiotic.",
    "intervention": "`symptomatic`, and it is the whole finding. **No treatment shortens acute bronchitis.** Antibiotics reduce cough duration by well under a day in meta-analysis, an effect small enough to be within the noise of how the endpoint is measured, and reliably increase adverse effects. Beta-agonists do not help patients without airflow obstruction. Antitussives have weak evidence. Honey is about as well supported as anything else. **The correct management is an explanation, a realistic expectation of how long the cough will last, and safety-netting.** That derives `capability: unsolved`, filing this beside diseases nobody knows what to do about — and the difference is that here everybody knows exactly what to do and does something else.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`. Hand hygiene, respiratory etiquette, influenza and COVID-19 vaccination and not smoking each remove some episodes. **There is nothing aimed at acute bronchitis specifically**, because it is a syndrome produced by a dozen unrelated viruses rather than an entity with a cause to vaccinate against.",
    "ongoing": "`none`, and it is one of very few records that can say so honestly. The illness ends, completely, in two to three weeks, and leaves nothing. **No residue block, no maintenance therapy, no follow-up** — which makes the contrast with the treatment burden the sharper: a disease with zero ongoing cost, routinely treated with a drug that has one.",
    "efficacy": "See the axis. The figure is deliberately low because crediting treatment with the natural history is the specific error this record exists to document.",
    "access": "`access` is 0.95 and means nothing. Everything recommended for this illness is free, universal and non-pharmaceutical. **The register's `reach = efficacy × access` construction assumes there is something to reach people with**, and this record shows what it does when there is not: it produces a low number for a benign, self-limiting, universally survivable disease and puts it near the bottom of a delivery chart."
   },
