     "kind": "cost",
     "blocks": "three hundred thousand dollars a year, for life, from childhood",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**A DISEASE FOUNDATION FUNDED THE RESEARCH, SOLD THE ROYALTIES FOR $3.3 BILLION, AND THE DRUG LISTS ABOVE $300,000 A YEAR.** Both halves are true and the register should hold them together. The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation's venture philanthropy model put roughly $150 million into the science when no company would, and it produced three approved drugs — the clearest demonstration in this corpus that a patient organisation can buy its own cure (gaps.md #188). It then sold the royalty stream, and the product was priced at a level that has produced multi-year standoffs between the manufacturer and national payers. **The standoffs are the specific harm, because this record has a window.** England's dispute over an earlier modulator ran for roughly three years before resolution; comparable negotiations elsewhere took years more. Modulators preserve lung function and do not restore it, so **every year of negotiation is permanent lung damage in a cohort of children**, and the register's `cost` blocker records a price rather than an accruing loss. Lifetime cost per patient runs into eight figures at list. That arithmetic is unavoidable in a chronic monogenic disease with a small population, and it is the central test case for whether orphan-drug pricing is sustainable when the orphan drug works.",
