     "blocks": "any mechanism for the idiopathic form, and any way to settle the criteria",
     "what": "The binding constraint, and it has an unusual shape: the missing knowledge is what would ADJUDICATE the definitional dispute, not merely what would treat the disease. A mechanism for idiopathic MCAS — a marker that identifies affected mast cells or affected patients independently of their symptoms — would decide which criteria set is selecting the right people, and would do so in a way that neither camp could argue with. Without it the dispute is between two symptom-plus-inference constructs and cannot be resolved by more of the same evidence. The precedent is encouraging and is recent. Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia was unknown before roughly 2016 and now explains part of what was idiopathic; KIT D816V did the same for the clonal group. Both were found by looking for a marker rather than by refining criteria. `scale: null` — no priced path exists.",
