     "blocks": "obtaining the one measurement that decides the diagnosis",
     "what": "Third variant of `diagnosis` in three consecutive records, and the one that shows the taxonomy entry is doing too much work. hEDS: no test exists, so the blocker is downstream of knowledge and money moves only part of it. POTS: the test exists, costs nothing, takes ten minutes, and is simply not performed — pure recognition, the most purchasable blocker in the register. HERE: the test exists, both camps agree it means something, and CAPTURING IT IS THE PROBLEM. It needs a baseline drawn when the patient is well and a second sample within about four hours of a spontaneous, unpredictable episode. That is a logistics problem with a biological deadline, and unusually it has a plausible engineering answer rather than a scientific one: patient-held sampling, point-of-care assay, or standing orders that let a patient present to any laboratory during an episode without a fresh referral. Priced modestly for that reason. It is the most tractable thing in this record and it would also, incidentally, supply the evidence to settle part of the dispute above.",
