     "blocks": "knowing before you cut who will keloid",
     "what": "**THIS IS A PROGNOSTIC GAP THAT SHOULD BE CHANGING SURGICAL DECISIONS EVERY DAY AND CHANGES NONE.** Nothing identifies, in advance of a wound, who will form a keloid. Ancestry and family history shift the prior and do not decide the case, so elective incisions, piercings and caesarean sections proceed and a share of the people who have them are left with a lesion worse than what was treated. **The value of even a mediocre test is unusually easy to state**: it would redirect incisions, add prophylaxis from day one — which is the one thing that reliably works — and let some elective procedures be declined by somebody who knew what they were choosing. It converts this disease from one that is treated badly into one that is largely prevented, and the window blocker below is the same fact. A direct instance of `who-progresses` (FND-O-0001), and among the most tractable in the corpus: the outcome is visible, appears within months, and the population having elective skin incisions is enormous. `scale: null`.",
