     "kind": "evidence-incomplete",
     "blocks": "not knowing which drug, and finding out only by failing",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**NOTHING PREDICTS WHICH ANTIPSYCHOTIC WILL WORK, AND THE ONLY ROUTE TO THE DRUG THAT DOES IS TO FAIL TWICE FIRST.** Selection among D2-blocking agents is by side-effect profile and formulary, because head-to-head efficacy differences are small. Each trial takes weeks to assess. **Treatment resistance is defined retrospectively — two adequate failed trials — so the criterion for clozapine can only be satisfied by spending a year or more not getting better**, during which functional and social losses accumulate that the `window` says are not recovered. What is missing is ordinary and answerable: a biomarker or clinical rule identifying likely non-responders at first episode, and trials of **clozapine first-line** in that group. Striatal dopamine synthesis capacity — lower in treatment-resistant patients — is the leading candidate and requires a PET scanner. **And the question of when treatment can be reduced or stopped is barely studied**, in a lifelong therapy causing metabolic disease that shortens life. A small number of discontinuation trials exist, they are contested, and the dominant clinical position is indefinite continuation with limited evidence about the tail. This is the same publicly-funded-comparison gap `multiple-sclerosis` and `covid-19` identified: nobody selling a product has a reason to fund it.",
