    {
     "date": 2013,
     "axis": "access",
     "from": 0.18,
     "to": 0.25,
     "why": "**ONE HEALTH SYSTEM TOOK CONTROL FROM 44% TO 90% AND PUBLISHED HOW.** A large American integrated care organisation raised hypertension control in its population from about 44% in 2001 to about 90% by 2013. The ingredients were unglamorous and are all reproducible: **a registry of every hypertensive patient**, a single simplified treatment algorithm, **single-pill combinations** to reduce the tablet count, free or near-free follow-up blood pressure checks done by medical assistants rather than physicians, and measurement of control as an organisational performance indicator. The World Health Organization's HEARTS technical package packages the same approach for low- and middle-income countries, and national programmes built on it have produced large control improvements in India, Cuba and elsewhere. **No discovery. No new drug. A registry, a protocol, a combination pill and somebody made responsible for the number.** This entry is dated to the published result rather than to any global change, because globally the figure remains about 21% — the demonstration exists and has not been copied.",
     "src": "recall"
    }
