     "kind": "logistics",
     "blocks": "half the people with it never being told",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**46% OF PEOPLE WITH THE WORLD'S LARGEST CAUSE OF DEATH DO NOT KNOW THEY HAVE IT**, and the test is a cuff. The disease produces no symptom, so nothing brings anybody in. Diagnosis happens opportunistically — at an unrelated appointment, an insurance medical, a pharmacy — and in much of the world there is no routine encounter at which to measure anybody. **The demonstration that this is solvable exists and is published.** A large integrated system took control from 44% to 90% with a registry, a single algorithm, single-pill combinations, and blood pressure checks performed by non-physicians. The WHO packages the same approach for low-resource settings and national programmes built on it have produced large gains. **The reason it has not spread is that every component is a running cost rather than a product** (gaps.md #154): somebody to maintain a register, somebody to take a blood pressure, somebody accountable for a control rate. There is nothing to procure, patent, ship or donate. This is the largest single delivery gap in the register by attributable deaths, and it is closed by administration.",
