     "blocks": "a symptomless condition treated with a daily tablet forever",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**ROUGHLY HALF OF PEOPLE STARTED ON ANTIHYPERTENSIVES ARE NOT TAKING THEM A YEAR LATER**, and the reason is structural rather than personal. The patient feels well before treatment and sometimes worse after it — a cough, swollen ankles, dizziness. The benefit is a probability shift in a disease they do not have, decades away, which they will never observe even if it works. **Nothing about persisting is reinforced by anything they can perceive** (gaps.md #161). What demonstrably helps is reducing the friction rather than increasing the motivation: **single-pill combinations**, which halve the tablet count and measurably improve control; free or subsidised medication, since even small co-payments reduce persistence; and longer prescription intervals. **And the thing that would change it most is pharmacological.** A six-monthly injectable — an interfering RNA against angiotensinogen is in mid-stage trials — converts adherence from a daily decision into a twice- yearly appointment. **In no other record in this register would that trade be worth as much**, because in no other record is the intervention this cheap, this effective and this widely abandoned.",
