     "what": "Global mean sodium intake is roughly twice the recommended maximum and **most of it is added during food manufacture**, not by the person eating — which makes individual advice close to useless and reformulation close to decisive. Population sodium reduction is one of the World Health Organization's \"best buys\". Mandatory reformulation targets work; voluntary ones work less well and more slowly. **Potassium-enriched salt substitution is the strongest recent evidence** — a large randomised trial in rural Chinese villages found reductions in stroke, major cardiovascular events and death simply from replacing the salt people already used. The obstacles are the familiar ones: reformulation costs manufacturers money, salt is a preservative and a flavour crutch, and the industry opposes mandatory targets — documented behaviour rather than a conspiracy claim. **The benefit is spread across this record, `stroke`, `ischaemic-heart-disease`, `chronic-kidney-disease` and gastric cancer, so no single record can justify the policy and nothing in the register sums it** (gaps.md #87). `who_could` has no food regulator.",
