     "blocks": "the number being wrong, and the curable cause never being sought",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**TWO FAILURES, AND THE SECOND IS A MISSED CURE AT SCALE.** *The measurement.* Office blood pressure misclassifies in both directions — white-coat hypertension in perhaps a fifth of those labelled, masked hypertension in perhaps an eighth of those cleared. Ambulatory and home monitoring predict outcomes better and are used inconsistently. **Cuff size errors alone can shift systolic readings by around 20 mmHg**, and mis-cuffing is routine. The instrument is objective and its application is not. *The curable cause nobody looks for.* **Primary aldosteronism plausibly accounts for 5-10% of all hypertension and up to a fifth of resistant hypertension, and is diagnosed in well under one percent of hypertensive patients.** It is found by a blood test — an aldosterone-to-renin ratio — and unilateral disease is cured by removing an adrenal gland. It also carries cardiovascular risk beyond its blood pressure. **A curable disease affecting tens of millions of people, identifiable by a cheap blood test, screened for almost never.** Nothing in this register is a better argument that the binding constraint in medicine is frequently not knowledge but the decision to look.",
