     "kind": "cost",
     "blocks": "a 1960s treatment that most of the world cannot buy",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**BETWEEN 2.3 AND 7.1 MILLION PEOPLE DIE EACH YEAR FOR WANT OF DIALYSIS, AND NOTHING ABOUT IT IS A SCIENTIFIC PROBLEM.** Haemodialysis costs on the order of tens of thousands of dollars a year per patient and must continue for life. In most low- and middle-income countries it is paid for out of pocket, which means it is available until a family's money runs out and then it stops — a pattern documented repeatedly, in which patients start treatment, impoverish their households, and discontinue. **Peritoneal dialysis is cheaper, needs no machine or dialysis centre, is done at home, and is used for a minority of patients almost everywhere** — including in wealthy systems where the reimbursement structure favours in-centre haemodialysis. Several countries have shown that a \"peritoneal-dialysis-first\" policy is both cheaper and at least as good. Local fluid manufacture is the main lever on its cost and is underdeveloped. **This is the register's largest gap between what a treatment costs to provide and what it is worth**, and unlike `#128`'s organ supply it is not capped by anything physical. Machines can be built and nurses trained. The constraint is that somebody has to decide to pay indefinitely for a population that will never stop needing it.",
