     "blocks": "a silent disease, two cheap tests, and most patients meeting a nephrologist too late",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THE TESTS COST ALMOST NOTHING AND THE HIGH-RISK POPULATION IS ALREADY IN THE CLINIC.** Serum creatinine and a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio identify most treatable chronic kidney disease. The people at highest risk — those with diabetes and hypertension — are already attending health services for those conditions. Screening them is recommended and inconsistently done, and albuminuria in particular is measured far less often than guidelines specify. The consequence is late referral, which is measured and consistently associated with worse survival, emergency dialysis starts through a catheter rather than a planned fistula, and much lower rates of transplant assessment and home therapy. **A patient who arrives late is routed into the worst modality by default and frequently stays there** — so this blocker produces the one below it. **And the instrument itself was a barrier until 2021.** The race coefficient in the estimating equations raised Black patients' apparent kidney function and delayed their referral and transplant listing for two decades (gaps.md",
