   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "none",
    "witness": "**DIAGNOSTIC is `objective` and there is a three-week door.** PCR on saliva or urine in the first two to three weeks of life establishes congenital infection definitively. **After that the test cannot distinguish congenital from postnatal acquisition**, and postnatal acquisition is common and harmless — so a diagnosis missed in the newborn period is usually unrecoverable, and dried blood spots retested later are insensitive. **That deadline is what makes the screening question decidable rather than academic**, and it is unusual: most of this register's diagnostic failures can be repaired late. In adults, viral load monitoring in transplant recipients is well established and drives pre-emptive therapy — one of the better-run surveillance programmes in medicine, and a striking contrast with the newborns. PROGNOSTIC is `partial`. Symptomatic infection at birth predicts poor outcome well. **The harder group is the roughly nine in ten who look normal at birth**, of whom a minority develop late-onset sensorineural hearing loss over the following years, and nothing identifies which — so all of them need audiological follow-up that most do not receive. PREDICTIVE is `none`. Antiviral resistance testing exists for transplant recipients; for the congenital question there is nothing that says which infant benefits from six months of a myelosuppressive drug."
   },
