   "residue": {
    "severity": "catastrophic",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**HEARING, AND A BRAIN.** Congenital CMV is the **leading non-genetic cause of childhood sensorineural hearing loss**, and the hearing loss is frequently progressive and sometimes late-onset — a child who passed the newborn hearing screen losing hearing at three or five. It also causes developmental delay, cerebral palsy, epilepsy, chorioretinitis and visual loss, and microcephaly. **This is `hearing-loss`'s largest preventable cause and that record does not know it**, because neither existed alongside the other until now. It also overlaps `cerebral-palsy`. Antiviral therapy improves these outcomes and does not restore them. **`toll` is rated `major` and `residue` `catastrophic` deliberately: the drug is unpleasant and reversible, and what the disease does is neither.**",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.2,
     "units": "fraction-of-congenitally-infected left with permanent hearing, neurological or visual impairment — reasoned across symptomatic and initially-asymptomatic infants, and the late-onset fraction is the part nobody counts well",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "`established`. Human cytomegalovirus is a betaherpesvirus transmitted in saliva, urine, breast milk, sexually and across the placenta, and — like the rest of its family — it **establishes lifelong latency**, here in cells of the **myeloid lineage** rather than in neurons. That is a different compartment from `genital-herpes` and `varicella-zoster`, and it is one reason the reservoir problem here has a different shape: the cells divide, and they are replaceable. **Young children are the reservoir that matters.** Toddlers shed CMV in saliva and urine for months, and the highest-risk exposure for a pregnant woman is her own toddler — which makes the prevention advice unusually specific and unusually hard to follow. **And the immunological fact that shapes everything: maternal seropositivity does not prevent congenital transmission.** In high-seroprevalence populations most congenital infection follows reactivation or reinfection with a different strain, so prior immunity reduces risk without abolishing it.",
    "intervention": "`disease-modifying`, and the two strata pull in opposite directions. **Nothing cures the latent virus.** For symptomatic congenital infection, six months of valganciclovir modestly improves hearing and neurodevelopment against six weeks, at the cost of neutropenia — a real effect, and a long way from restoration. For transplant recipients, monitoring with pre-emptive therapy works, and **letermovir prophylaxis since 2017 is genuinely good**. The rung reflects the congenital stratum because that is where the burden is.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`, and **it is thin enough to be uncomfortable given the burden.** There is no vaccine. What exists is hygiene advice to pregnant women — not sharing food or utensils with a toddler, handwashing after nappy changes, avoiding kissing a young child on the mouth — which trials suggest reduces seroconversion and which **most pregnant women are never given**, because most have never heard of the infection. Antenatal screening is not standard almost anywhere; newborn screening is increasingly discussed and is done in a few places, sometimes triggered by a failed hearing screen rather than universally. **The screening question here has a harder edge than `toxoplasmosis`'s**, because the diagnostic window closes at about three weeks and because a treatment with a real effect exists.",
    "ongoing": "`low` for the record. Latent infection carries no burden for the four billion people who have it. **The child with congenital CMV carries a lifetime of audiological follow-up, hearing aids or implants, and developmental support** — which is `residue` rather than treatment, and is the whole cost.",
    "efficacy": "See the axis. No figure describes clearing the infection, and the congenital figure describes improvement rather than prevention.",
    "access": "**The binding constraint is that nobody looks within three weeks.** PCR on saliva or urine is cheap, fast and definitive; after about three weeks it cannot distinguish congenital from postnatal infection, and the opportunity is gone permanently. Most countries do no newborn CMV screening at all. In transplantation the picture inverts completely: viral load surveillance is routine, letermovir is available, and outcomes are good. **Two populations, one virus, and the difference is entirely whether a programme exists.**"
   },
