   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "objective",
    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "partial",
    "witness": "**DIAGNOSTIC IS `objective` AND HAS BEEN FOR A CENTURY.** Pure-tone audiometry quantifies the loss by frequency and by ear; tympanometry separates conductive from sensorineural; and there is no diagnostic ambiguity worth arguing about. **NEWBORN HEARING SCREENING IS THE PART THAT MATTERS AND IT IS A TWO-MINUTE TEST ON A SLEEPING BABY.** Otoacoustic emissions or automated auditory brainstem response, done before discharge, detects congenital hearing loss in the first days of life. The international benchmark is **\"1-3-6\"** — screened by one month, diagnosed by three, intervention by six — and where it is met the outcomes for deaf children are transformed. It is universal in most high-income countries and largely absent elsewhere. PROGNOSTIC is `partial`. Audiometric configuration predicts device benefit reasonably; what it predicts poorly is **who will use the device**, which is the outcome that actually determines whether anything happened. PREDICTIVE is `partial`. Duration of deafness before cochlear implantation predicts speech outcome — longer auditory deprivation, worse result — and in children, age at implantation predicts spoken language strongly. **Nothing predicts an individual's satisfaction with a hearing aid**, which is why around a third to a half of dispensed devices end up unworn. **And the measurement this record most needs is not audiometric.** A pure-tone average says nothing about understanding speech in a noisy room, which is what people actually complain of and what devices handle worst."
   },
   "toll": {
    "severity": "minor",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**THE DEVICES ARE BENIGN AND THE HISTORY IS NOT.** A hearing aid carries essentially no medical risk. **Cochlear implantation** is a surgical procedure with the usual small risks — facial nerve injury, wound problems, device failure requiring reimplantation — plus two specific to it: an elevated risk of **bacterial meningitis**, which is why pneumococcal vaccination is required before implantation, and **loss of any residual natural hearing in the implanted ear**, which is permanent and is the reason the `permanent` flag is set on an otherwise mild toll. **THE TOLL THIS RECORD CARRIES THAT NO FIELD HOLDS IS HISTORICAL AND EDUCATIONAL.** From the late nineteenth century, deaf education in much of the world adopted **oralism** — the suppression of sign language in favour of forced speech and lip-reading — following an international congress in 1880 that voted to exclude signing from schools. Generations of deaf children were punished for using their language, and the documented result was worse education, worse literacy and worse outcomes, not better speech. That is a medical and educational intervention that harmed a population for a century, it is the direct historical basis of the contestation in `witness.contested`, and **`toll` has no field for it** — it was not a treatment side effect, it was the treatment.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.03,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated (permanent harm — chiefly loss of residual hearing after cochlear implantation, and device complications) — a reasoned estimate",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "residue": {
    "severity": "major",
    "permanent": true,
    "what": "**THE PERMANENT DAMAGE IN THIS RECORD IS NOT TO HEARING. IT IS TO LANGUAGE, AND IT HAPPENS IN THE FIRST FEW YEARS.** Human language acquisition has a critical period. A child who receives no accessible language input in the first years — no amplified speech they can resolve, **and no sign language either** — shows lasting deficits in language, literacy, academic attainment and aspects of cognition, and later intervention does not fully recover them. The phenomenon has a name, **language deprivation**, and it is the single most consequential fact in paediatric audiology. **The crucial and frequently elided point is that hearing is one of two ways to prevent it.** A deaf child raised with fluent sign language from infancy has full language and no deprivation. A deaf child given a cochlear implant early and good rehabilitation may have full spoken language. **A deaf child given neither — which is the global default — has neither**, and that is the outcome the residue figure below is about. In adults the permanence is different and softer: prolonged auditory deprivation degrades central auditory processing, which is why late cochlear implantation gives worse speech understanding than early, and why the eight-to- ten-year delay described in the `adherence` blocker has a cost beyond the years lost.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.15,
     "units": "fraction-of-affected (permanent language, literacy or cognitive consequence of deprivation, or irreversible auditory deprivation effects) — a reasoned estimate concentrated in children who received neither early amplification nor early sign language",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
   "window": {
    "what": "**THE WINDOW CLOSES ON LANGUAGE, NOT ON HEARING — AND THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO KEEP IT OPEN.** That distinction is the whole of this record's clinical ethics and it is routinely collapsed. The developmental deadline is the critical period for language acquisition: a child needs **accessible linguistic input** in the first years or the capacity is permanently degraded. **Accessible** can mean amplified or implanted speech, and it can mean a fluent sign language. Both work. Neither is the default in most of the world. The clinical timings follow from it. Newborn screening by one month, diagnosis by three, intervention by six. Cochlear implantation before about twelve months produces markedly better spoken language than implantation at three years. Every month of delay in the first years costs more than a year of delay at thirty. **In adults there is a second, softer window.** People wait eight to ten years from noticing hearing loss to obtaining a hearing aid, and duration of deafness before intervention predicts how much benefit a device delivers — the auditory system deteriorates centrally when it is not used. **The delay is not neutral, and almost everybody treats it as if it were.** And a third, disputed one: if hearing loss contributes causally to dementia, then intervening earlier matters for a reason nobody has established. See `witness.prevention`.",
    "closes_on": "the critical period for language acquisition in early childhood — a few years, and it closes on language rather than on hearing; and in adults, progressive central auditory deprivation",
    "caught_in_time": {
     "value": 0.3,
     "units": "fraction-of-cases (children reaching accessible language, spoken or signed, within the critical period; adults intervening before substantial deprivation) — a reasoned estimate, high where newborn screening exists and near zero elsewhere",
     "src": "reasoning"
    }
   },
