   "measurement": {
    "diagnostic": "clinical",
    "prognostic": "none",
    "predictive": "none",
    "witness": "The worst measurement record in the register, on all three questions at once. No objective diagnostic test. No way to predict course, chronicity or recurrence in an individual. And no biomarker to choose among treatments, so a patient may spend months cycling through agents — the STAR*D design was not a study artefact but a description of practice. Every effective treatment was found rather than designed, and none of them can be aimed."
   },
   "toll": {
    "severity": "minor",
    "permanent": false,
    "what": "Mostly reversible: sexual dysfunction, weight change and emotional blunting on SSRIs, with discontinuation syndromes that can be prolonged, and a contested question over whether sexual dysfunction persists after stopping in a small minority. ECT causes retrograde amnesia that is usually but not always transient. Nothing here is amputation, and nothing is nothing.",
    "incidence": {
     "value": 0.05,
     "units": "fraction-of-treated (persistent harm)",
     "src": "recall"
    }
   },
   "witness": {
    "mechanism": "Rated `correlates`, and this is the rating most likely to be objected to, so the grounds should be explicit. There is no established causal chain from any initiating cause to the syndrome. The monoamine/serotonin-deficiency account was inferred backwards from drug action and does not survive as an explanation — the 2022 umbrella review of serotonin measures found no consistent deficiency, and the timing problem (receptor occupancy in hours, clinical effect in weeks) was never resolved. What exists is a large set of reproducible associations: HPA-axis findings, inflammatory markers, hippocampal volume, heritability around 30-40%, and hundreds of GWAS loci of tiny individual effect. Associations without a chain is exactly what `correlates` denotes.",
    "intervention": "Rated `symptomatic` deliberately. SSRIs, SNRIs, and psychotherapy produce real but modest benefit over placebo; STAR*D found roughly a third remitting at the first step and about two-thirds cumulatively across four steps, with substantial relapse. ECT is genuinely highly effective in severe and treatment-resistant cases and its mechanism is even less understood than the drugs'. Ketamine and esketamine act within hours, which falsifies the slow-monoamine story and does not replace it. Nothing here is known to alter an underlying process, because no underlying process is specified — so `disease-modifying` cannot be claimed even where outcomes improve.",
    "prevention": "`risk-reduction`. Psychological prevention programmes and treatment of adversity show modest effects; there is no prophylaxis.",
    "ongoing": "`moderate` — long-term medication with side effects that drive discontinuation, high recurrence, and a chronic or recurrent course in a large minority."
   },
   "holes": [
    "Whether MDD is one entity is unresolved, and if it is not, every number in this file is an average over unlike things. This is the register's most consequential open question about a high-burden entry.",
    "No objective diagnostic marker, so treatment allocation is trial and error.",
    "The mechanism of every effective treatment, including the fast-acting ones and ECT, is unknown. `intervention` improving without `mechanism` moving is the definition of this quadrant and should be watched as a pattern."
   ],
