     "blocks": "any way to screen a compound against the organism at all",
     "what": "**THE MOST UPSTREAM `tooling` BLOCKER IN THE REGISTER.** For decades *Cryptosporidium* could not be maintained in continuous culture, and there is no small-animal model that reproduces human disease in an immunocompetent host. **So there was no assay.** A drug-discovery programme needs something to test compounds against, and this organism did not provide one — which is a large part of why an infection identified in humans in 1976 still has one weak drug. That is changing: air-liquid-interface intestinal cultures, stem-cell-derived organoids and hollow-fibre systems now support the full life cycle, and genetic modification became possible in the mid-2010s. **The blocker is being cleared by instruments rather than by insight**, which is exactly the class of capability gaps.md #164 says this register holds no way to express — it records interventions, not the tools that produce them. The register's other ten `tooling` blockers are about steering or evaluating a treatment and each presumes an assay exists. **This one is the rung below that**, which is why it is worth distinguishing. `scale` is priced because building culture systems and screening libraries is buyable work of a very ordinary kind.",
