     "blocks": "knowing whether preventing the infection prevents the stunting",
     "what": "Birth-cohort studies associate cryptosporidial infection — **including infection with no diarrhoea** — with reduced linear growth and later cognitive deficit. If that is causal it may exceed the mortality burden, and it would change what this disease is for: not an episode of diarrhoea but a determinant of how a child develops. **No intervention has shown that preventing or treating the infection prevents the deficit**, and the confounding is severe — cryptosporidiosis tracks poverty, crowding, unsafe water, coinfection and prior malnutrition, each of which independently stunts children. The trial is hard because the only available drug does not work in this population, so the natural design would have to be a water or hygiene intervention with growth as the endpoint. **This is the register's clearest case of a burden that is probably large and is not established**, and it is filed here rather than asserted in the witness for that reason.",
