     "blocks": "one to two years, out of three to five",
     "what": "**THE DELAY IS A THIRD OF THE ILLNESS AND THE FINDING THAT WOULD SHORTEN IT IS AUDIBLE WITH A STETHOSCOPE.** Median time from symptom onset to diagnosis runs to one to two years. Progressive breathlessness in an older person is attributed to COPD, heart failure, deconditioning or age; the chest radiograph is frequently unremarkable or reported as non-specific chronic change; and nobody orders the high-resolution CT that would settle it until other things have failed. **Fine, dry, velcro-like inspiratory crackles at both lung bases are characteristic, present early, and detectable in a primary care consultation room** — which makes physical examination the cheapest intervention available in this record and one that has quietly fallen out of use. **Then the diagnosis itself needs a service.** High-resolution CT, an expert thoracic radiologist, and a multidisciplinary meeting; expert centres disagree with one another often enough for it to have been measured, and most of the world has no such meeting to convene. **Because antifibrotics do not recover lost lung, the delay is not merely inconvenient — it is subtraction.** A patient diagnosed two years late starts treatment permanently worse off, and that is the strongest argument for the unglamorous fix: teach the crackles, lower the threshold for CT.",
