     "blocks": "knowing whether the drugs extend life, and what to do in an exacerbation",
     "what": "**THE TWO DRUGS THAT DEFINE THIS RECORD WERE LICENSED ON A SURROGATE AND THE QUESTION CAN NO LONGER BE ASKED.** Forced vital capacity decline is a reasonable surrogate and it is a surrogate. Neither pivotal trial was powered for mortality; pooled analyses, open-label extensions and registries support a survival benefit. **A placebo-controlled mortality trial would now be unethical**, so the strongest available evidence is the strongest evidence there will ever be. **This is gaps.md #102's family again** — a question whose answer is foreclosed — and it arises here not from rarity but from success: **approval on a surrogate removed the possibility of testing the endpoint that matters.** `alzheimers` records the same structure less comfortably. **And acute exacerbations are the unaddressed half.** They cause roughly half of the deaths in this disease and have no established treatment. High dose corticosteroids are given by convention; the evidence base is observational and weak; and the 2012 finding that steroids increased mortality in stable disease makes that convention actively uneasy. **A randomised trial in acute exacerbation is hard, necessary, and has not been done.** Third: **nothing distinguishes the two available drugs.** No head-to-head trial exists, combination is untested, and a physician chooses between diarrhoea and photosensitivity on the patient's preference.",
