     "obstacles": [],
     "blocks": "two drugs that are priced like orphan therapies",
     "what": "**BOTH ANTIFIBROTICS WERE PRICED AS ORPHAN DRUGS FOR A DISEASE WITH THREE MILLION PATIENTS.** Annual cost per patient runs to tens of thousands of dollars in high-income markets. Reimbursement is frequently restricted by lung function criteria — treat only within a defined FVC band — which is a rule that **withholds a drug from the patients with the most lung left to protect**, given that the drugs slow decline and cannot recover it. Generic entry has begun for pirfenidone in some markets and has not yet moved the picture globally. **And the access failure upstream is larger than the price.** Most of the world has neither the high-resolution CT nor the multidisciplinary service needed to make the diagnosis, so the drug's cost is not the binding constraint for the majority of the three million — **not being diagnosed is.** **Lung transplantation is the other cost and it is not money.** It is donor organs, and the number is fixed by something other than budgets. IPF is now among the commonest indications for lung transplant, which means this record competes directly with every other end-stage lung disease for the same fixed supply — gaps.md #128's zero-sum shape.",
