     "blocks": "eighty-seven percent of infected people not knowing",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "A one-dollar fingerprick test for hepatitis B surface antigen has existed for decades and is definitive. **Roughly 13% of the world's 254 million infected people have been diagnosed.** The disease is silent for thirty years, which removes the usual prompt to seek testing; there is no symptom to bring anybody in. Where screening does happen it is opportunistic — antenatal clinics, blood donation, migration health checks — and each of those finds a slice of a population and misses the rest. Stigma is real and documented, including employment and marriage discrimination in several high-prevalence countries, and it makes a positive result costly to hold in a setting where no treatment follows it. **That last clause matters.** Testing without a treatment pathway produces a diagnosis and nothing else, which is a poor bargain to offer somebody, and it is why this blocker and the two on either side of it are really one problem broken into three.",
