    "prognostic": "partial",
    "predictive": "partial",
    "witness": "**DIAGNOSTIC IS `clinical` AND THE DELAY IS A THIRD OF THE ILLNESS.** There is no test. Diagnosis is a clinician finding upper and lower motor neuron signs across body regions, supported by electromyography, with MRI and bloods used to **exclude the things it might otherwise be**. The criteria have been revised repeatedly — El Escorial, Awaji, and the 2020 Gold Coast criteria — each attempting to shorten a diagnostic process that is fundamentally one of exclusion. **Median time from first symptom to diagnosis is around ten to sixteen months.** Against a median survival of thirty, that is a third to a half of the illness spent undiagnosed, and it is the reason `window` below matters more than it does in most records. PROGNOSTIC is `partial` and better than it used to be. Site of onset (bulbar is worse), age, rate of decline over the first months, respiratory function and the ALSFRS-R slope predict survival at group level with real reliability. **What is not predicted is the long tail** — a minority live a decade or more and nobody can identify them at diagnosis, which is exactly the person the question is being asked about. PREDICTIVE is `partial`, and it rests almost entirely on **genotype**. Knowing a patient carries a *SOD1* mutation selects tofersen; nothing else here selects anything. **Neurofilament light chain** is the interesting development — a blood and CSF marker of axonal damage that tracks disease activity, that fell substantially on tofersen, and **on which that drug was granted accelerated approval.** It is the register's clearest instance of a biomarker being accepted as a surrogate endpoint in neurodegeneration, and whether it is a good one is not settled."
