     "date": 2024,
     "axis": "efficacy",
     "from": 0.22,
     "to": 0.2,
     "why": "**A BACKWARDS ENTRY, AND IT IS THE SYSTEM WORKING RATHER THAN FAILING.** AMX0035 (sodium phenylbutyrate–taurursodiol) was approved in 2022 on a small phase 2 trial with a supportive survival analysis, under considerable and public patient advocacy. **Its confirmatory phase 3 trial missed on every endpoint, and the manufacturer withdrew the drug from the market in 2024.** **Record it as a success of the mechanism it is usually cited against.** Accelerated approval carries a confirmatory requirement; the requirement was honoured, the trial was run, it was negative, and the product was withdrawn. Compare `alzheimers`, where the analogous story has been much messier. **And record the cost honestly too**, because it is not zero: patients took a drug that did not work, at expense, in place of trial participation, and the community that fought for the approval then had to absorb the withdrawal. **This register's `access` figure fell because a treatment was removed, and that was the right thing to do** — which is a shape the register has recorded only for `osteoporosis` and `low-back-pain`, and in both of those cases the withdrawal was the *wrong* decision or an overcorrection. This one was correct.",
