     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**TEN TO SIXTEEN MONTHS FROM FIRST SYMPTOM TO DIAGNOSIS, AGAINST A MEDIAN SURVIVAL OF THIRTY.** There is no test. Diagnosis is a specialist recognising upper and lower motor neuron signs across regions and excluding everything else, and the route there typically runs through an orthopaedic surgeon, a physiotherapist or an ENT clinic first, because early ALS presents as a dropped foot, a weak hand or a hoarse voice. **What the delay costs is irreversible.** Motor neurons lost before diagnosis are gone; non-invasive ventilation started before respiratory failure is established extends survival more than any drug here; and trial eligibility usually requires being diagnosed while still well enough to qualify — **so the delay also determines who the evidence is generated on**, and systematically excludes the fastest-progressing patients from the trials meant to help them. **A blood test would change this record more than any drug currently in development.** Neurofilament light is elevated in ALS and is not specific to it; it is used for monitoring rather than diagnosis. A specific diagnostic marker is a tractable, unglamorous target that would buy months of treatable illness in every patient.",
