     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THE INTERVENTIONS THAT EXTEND SURVIVAL MOST IN THIS DISEASE ARE A MULTIDISCIPLINARY CLINIC AND A VENTILATOR, AND NEITHER SCALES.** A functioning ALS service is a neurologist, a respiratory physician, a specialist nurse, physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy, dietetics, palliative care and access to communication technology — coordinated, and reaching a patient who is losing the ability to travel to it. Attendance at such a clinic is associated with longer survival than any medication in this record. **They exist in a minority of high-income centres and essentially nowhere else.** **Non-invasive ventilation is the single most effective intervention and it requires equipment, titration and support at home.** Gastrostomy requires a procedure timed before respiratory function makes it unsafe — a window that is routinely missed. **And communication technology is the one that decides what the illness is like.** Eye-gaze systems let a person who cannot move or speak continue to be present in their own life; they are expensive, funded inconsistently, and provided late. **Across most of the world the diagnosis is not made and none of this applies**, which is why `access` reads 0.35 rather than something worse: the figure is dominated by the countries that have services at all.",
