     "why": "**A NATIONAL BAN, AND YOU CAN SEE IT IN THE MORTALITY CURVE TWENTY YEARS LATER.** The United Kingdom prohibited all remaining asbestos use in 1999; the European Union followed in 2005; around seventy countries have now banned it. **UK mesothelioma deaths peaked around 2019 and have begun to fall** — two decades after the ban, exactly as a twenty-to-fifty-year latency predicts. **It is one of the cleanest demonstrations of primary prevention in this register and it is invisible on any short timescale.** A policy whose effect appears two decades after it is enacted is one no electoral cycle can evaluate, and the register's `moved` field — which pairs a date with an effect — is the only place this shape can be recorded at all. **The rating is `prophylaxis` rather than `risk-reduction` deliberately**: removing the exposure does not reduce the risk of asbestos-caused mesothelioma, it eliminates it. Roughly a fifth of cases are not attributable to asbestos and remain.",
