     "why": "**A MAJOR OPERATION WAS TESTED AND FOUND NOT TO HELP, AND STOPPING IT IS THE PROGRESS.** Radical surgery for pleural mesothelioma has been performed for decades in two forms — extrapleural pneumonectomy, which removes the lung, and extended pleurectomy decortication, which spares it. **Randomised evidence did not support the first, and a randomised trial reporting in 2024 found the second did not improve survival either, with more serious adverse events in the surgical arm.** **This is a toll reduction achieved by subtraction**, and it is the fourth such entry the register has recorded in a month — after `head-neck-cancer`'s organ preservation and IMRT, and `nasopharyngeal-carcinoma`'s IMRT. **`toll.severity` does not move, for the same reason it did not move in any of them**: the severe cases still exist, `terms` reads severity, and the register cannot say a toll became rarer without becoming milder (gaps.md #183). **Four entries in four records now, and it is the most frequently unrecordable kind of progress in this corpus.** Note also what it is not: the surgery was not a fraud or a fringe practice. It was mainstream, offered in specialist centres, and believed in — and it took two randomised trials across two decades to establish that it should not be done.",
