     "what": "**THE CAUSE OF FOUR IN FIVE CASES IS STILL IN COMMERCIAL PRODUCTION.** Around 1.3 million tonnes of chrysotile asbestos are mined each year, principally in Russia and Kazakhstan, and used principally in construction materials across South and South-East Asia. Roughly seventy countries have banned all forms. **The largest producers, the largest consumers and several of the fastest-growing construction markets have not.** **Chrysotile has repeatedly been blocked from Annex III of the Rotterdam Convention**, which would require exporting countries only to obtain the prior informed consent of importers — a disclosure requirement rather than a prohibition. The blocking has been done by a small number of producing and importing states, and the convention operates by consensus. **THE STANDING IS `disputed` AND THAT IS THE POINT OF RECORDING IT.** This blocker is not agreed to be a blocker. The chrysotile industry and several governments hold that the serpentine mineral is materially less hazardous than the amphiboles that caused the historical epidemic, that its biopersistence is far lower, and that **controlled use** under modern industrial hygiene is acceptable. WHO and IARC hold that all forms cause mesothelioma with no established safe threshold. **The register records that the dispute exists, who holds each position, and that a commercial interest is attached to one of them** — and does not adjudicate the mineralogy. This is the **second `disputed` blocker in 449**, and the register's stated reason for having the field is to test the *\"it's solvable but somebody won't allow it\"* refrain rather than to echo it. **Here the refrain is substantially true and the counter-argument is real**, which is exactly the case the axis was built for. **And the legacy half is not disputed by anybody.** Asbestos already installed in buildings across every country that used it is a continuing exposure hazard during renovation and demolition, it is nobody's clear responsibility, and it is why tradespeople are now the highest-risk occupational group in banned-asbestos countries.",
