     "blocks": "tissue, a specialist pathologist, and months",
     "what": "**THE DIAGNOSIS NEEDS A THORACOSCOPIC BIOPSY AND A PATHOLOGIST WHO HAS SEEN IT BEFORE.** Pleural fluid cytology is frequently insufficient. Distinguishing malignant mesothelioma from **reactive mesothelial hyperplasia** — benign, and morphologically similar — and from metastatic adenocarcinoma requires an immunohistochemical panel and, increasingly, BAP1 and CDKN2A testing. **In a district hospital without thoracic surgery and specialist pathology, this diagnosis is not made.** **Which is why the burden figure is an undercount of unknown size.** Mesothelioma is nearly pathognomonic for asbestos exposure and therefore the sentinel by which asbestos harm is measured — **so a country without the pathology to diagnose it also has no evidence of its own asbestos problem**, and that circularity is doing real work in the policy dispute above. **The delay for individuals is months** against a median survival of eighteen, because breathlessness from an effusion is investigated as infection or heart failure first. **What would help is not a new test so much as pathology capacity and referral pathways** in the countries where incidence is rising — which is unglamorous, cheap relative to the alternatives, and would also produce the surveillance data that the asbestos debate currently lacks.",
