    "note": "**PREVALENCE ROUGHLY TRIPLED SINCE THE 1970s AND THE HUMAN GENOME DID NOT CHANGE.** That single observation is the strongest argument in this record and it points the whole file away from individual behaviour and towards the food environment: whatever happened, happened to everybody at once, over about two generations, in almost every country. **AND THE COUNT DEPENDS ON A CUT-POINT WITH AN ETHNICITY TERM IN IT.** Obesity is conventionally BMI ≥30, and the World Health Organization recommends lower action points — around 23 and 27.5 — for Asian populations, because cardiometabolic risk appears at lower BMI where body composition differs. So the same body is obese in Delhi and overweight in Copenhagen, and unlike the kidney equation's race coefficient **this correction was kept, deliberately, because removing it would under-treat the people it applies to** (gaps.md"
