     "from": "none",
     "to": "suppressive",
     "why": "**BARIATRIC SURGERY BECAME LEGITIMATE MEDICINE, AND IT IS STILL THE MOST EFFECTIVE THING IN THIS RECORD.** A national consensus conference in 1991 established surgical treatment as appropriate for severe obesity, ending a period in which it had been regarded as a fringe and dangerous intervention. What followed — Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, then sleeve gastrectomy — produces weight reduction on the order of 25-30% sustained for many years, along with remission of type 2 diabetes in a large fraction and reduced long-term mortality in cohort studies. **It reaches one or two percent of the people who qualify for it**, which is gaps.md #174's problem — the better and more durable option losing to the one that is easier to start. **Note the rung.** Even surgery is `suppressive`, not `curative`: weight regain occurs over years in a substantial minority, and the anatomy is permanently altered with lifelong nutritional consequences.",
     "src": "recall"
