     "what": "**THE ARITHMETIC DOES NOT WORK AND EVERYBODY KNOWS IT.** List prices for the effective agents run around a thousand dollars a month in the United States, lower elsewhere, and the treatment does not end. Roughly 890 million adults have obesity. **No health system on earth can fund indefinite therapy at that price for that denominator**, and the cost-effectiveness question is genuinely different from any other drug in this register because the eligible population is a substantial fraction of all adults. Three things are moving it. **Competition** — several agents now, more in late-stage trials, and an oral small-molecule agonist would change manufacturing economics entirely, since peptide supply constraints produced real shortages between 2022 and 2024. **Patent expiry**, beginning for semaglutide in some jurisdictions in the coming years. And **negotiated pricing**, with the first agreements substantially below list. **The distributional point is the sharp one.** Obesity prevalence is highest in the most deprived populations in most high-income countries, and a thousand-dollar-a-month drug will reach the least deprived first. **A treatment that works and is priced this way widens the gap it was meant to close**, and this register has no field that records an intervention making an inequality worse.",
