     "kind": "knowledge",
     "blocks": "knowing what causes the dementia, having established what causes the plaques",
     "obstacles": [],
     "what": "**THE AMYLOID HYPOTHESIS WAS RIGHT AND IT WAS NOT ENOUGH, WHICH IS A HARDER POSITION THAN BEING WRONG.** Genetics establishes that Aβ initiates the disease — dominant mutations, trisomy 21, and a protective variant that lowers Aβ production. Clearing plaque slows decline, which confirms it prospectively. And clearing plaque slows decline by roughly a quarter, in the earliest patients, and does nothing at all once dementia is moderate. So the operative questions are downstream and open: **how tau propagates between neurons and why it tracks cognition when amyloid does not; what role neuroinflammation and microglia play; why some people carry a full plaque and tangle burden with no impairment** — the resilience question, which is the most useful one nobody can answer; and how the several co-occurring pathologies of the very old interact. A meaningful part of the field now argues the amyloid era consumed two decades and enormous resources on a target that is necessary, insufficient, and largely spent by the time anybody presents. **The prevention trials in asymptomatic amyloid-positive people are the decisive test**, and their readouts will either make this record a screening problem or close the chapter. `scale: null`.",
