     "obstacles": [],
     "blocks": "recognising in weeks a disease most clinicians never see",
     "what": "**RT-QuIC WORKS AND MOST OF THE WORLD CANNOT ORDER IT.** The assay is excellent and is performed in a small number of national reference laboratories. Outside them, diagnosis remains clinical, slow, and frequently reached only at autopsy — which matters less for the patient, who has no treatment either way, and enormously for **surveillance, infection control and the families of genetic cases.** **The clinical problem underneath it is unfixable by any test.** A neurologist may see one case in a career. Rapidly progressive dementia has a long differential, several entries of which are treatable — autoimmune encephalitis above all — so the diagnostic process is properly a search for the things that are not this. **In a disease measured in months, that search consumes most of the illness.** **What a test would buy is not survival but everything else**: an end to the investigation, time to plan, eligibility for trials, correct infection control for any subsequent surgery, and — in genetic cases — the beginning of a conversation with a family. The register has no field that records any of it.",
