     "what": "This is where the fifty-nine thousand deaths are, and the shape of it is unusual enough to state carefully. A full post-exposure course costs on the order of tens of dollars where the patients earn a few dollars a day, and rabies immunoglobulin — needed for severe exposures and the component that does the most for the worst bites — is scarce, expensive and frequently simply unavailable. Out-of-pocket payment for a bite is a documented cause of catastrophic household expense, and of people abandoning the course after one or two doses. AND THE MONEY IS BEING SPENT ON THE WRONG THING, unavoidably. Prophylaxis is reactive, expensive, and must be given to everyone plausibly exposed — twenty-nine million a year — while dog vaccination is a dollar or two per animal and removes the exposures. **You cannot stop funding the first to pay for the second, because the first is what keeps people alive in the meantime.** The register treats blockers as independent and these two are substitutes; see gaps.md #81.",
